// 科目题库：英语（由 exam-bank.js 拆分而来，内容未改动）
window.SUBJECT_BANK = window.SUBJECT_BANK || {};
window.SUBJECT_BANK['english'] = {"id":"english","name":"英语","chapters":[{"id":"english-c1","name":"词汇"},{"id":"english-c2","name":"情景"},{"id":"english-c3","name":"语法"},{"id":"english-c4","name":"阅读"}],"questions":[{"id":"english-vocab-1","origNo":1,"type":"single","stem":"The new policy will ______ the living standards of the people in rural areas.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"affect"},{"key":"B","text":"effect"},{"key":"C","text":"afford"},{"key":"D","text":"effort"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：这项新政策将影响农村地区人民的生活水平。\n【生词】policy 政策；living standard 生活水平；rural 农村的。\n【选项】A. affect vt. 影响（动词）——符合\"政策影响生活\"的语境，正确。B. effect n. 影响 / vt. 促成——作名词才是\"影响\"，作动词意为\"促成（变化等）\"，此处需动词\"影响\"，故不选。C. afford vt. 买得起、负担得起——通常接物品或费用（afford a car），不合语境，故不选。D. effort n. 努力——是名词，不能作谓语动词，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-2","origNo":2,"type":"single","stem":"She has made up her mind to ______ the task even if it means working overtime.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"accomplish"},{"key":"B","text":"accompany"},{"key":"C","text":"accumulate"},{"key":"D","text":"accommodate"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：即使要加班，她也已下定决心完成这项任务。\n【生词】make up one's mind 下决心；task 任务；work overtime 加班。\n【选项】A. accomplish vt. 完成（任务）——与 task 搭配，符合\"下决心完成\"的语境，正确。B. accompany vt. 陪伴、陪同——语义不符，故不选。C. accumulate v. 积累——通常接财富/经验，不合语境，故不选。D. accommodate vt. 容纳、提供住宿——语义不符，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-3","origNo":3,"type":"single","stem":"The manager was ______ of stealing company funds and was later dismissed.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"accused"},{"key":"B","text":"charged"},{"key":"C","text":"blamed"},{"key":"D","text":"scolded"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：这位经理被指控盗取公司资金，随后被解雇。\n【生词】accuse 控告；steal 盗取；funds 资金；dismiss 解雇。\n【选项】A. accuse vt. 指控——be accused of（被指控……）为固定搭配，正确。B. charge vt. 控告——搭配为 be charged with，与空后的 of 不搭配，故不选。C. blame vt. 责备——搭配为 blame sb for sth，不合此处结构，故不选。D. scold vt. 斥责——指口头责骂，语气和搭配都不合，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-4","origNo":4,"type":"single","stem":"The old building was completely ______ by fire last night, leaving nothing but ashes.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"damaged"},{"key":"B","text":"ruined"},{"key":"C","text":"destroyed"},{"key":"D","text":"hurt"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：昨晚那栋旧建筑被大火彻底摧毁，只留下灰烬。\n【生词】completely 彻底地；destroy 摧毁；ash 灰烬。\n【选项】A. damage vt. 损害——程度较轻，可修复，与\"只留灰烬\"矛盾，故不选。B. ruin vt. 毁坏——程度重，但多指抽象或逐渐毁坏，不如 destroy 彻底，故不选。C. destroy vt. 彻底摧毁——破坏程度最重，与\"leaving nothing but ashes（只留灰烬）\"最吻合，正确。D. hurt vt. 伤害——多用于人或身体，不用于建筑物，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-5","origNo":5,"type":"single","stem":"You should ______ the opportunity to learn from experienced colleagues while you are still a new employee.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"take"},{"key":"B","text":"make"},{"key":"C","text":"get"},{"key":"D","text":"seize"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：趁你还是新员工，你应该抓住向有经验的同事学习的机会。\n【生词】opportunity 机会；experienced 有经验的；colleague 同事；employee 员工。\n【选项】A. take vt. 拿、取——可与 opportunity 搭配，但语气平淡，不如 seize 强调\"主动把握\"，故不选。B. make vt. 制作——make the opportunity 多指\"创造机会\"，与\"已有机会\"语境略偏，故不选。C. get vt. 得到——get the opportunity 偏\"获得机会\"，不如 seize 贴切，故不选。D. seize vt. 抓住——seize the opportunity（抓住机会）为固定搭配，最契合\"珍惜、主动把握\"，正确。故选D。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-6","origNo":6,"type":"single","stem":"The conference has been ______ until next Friday due to the unexpected absence of the keynote speaker.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"put off"},{"key":"B","text":"put away"},{"key":"C","text":"put out"},{"key":"D","text":"put up"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：由于主讲人意外缺席，会议已推迟到下周五。\n【生词】conference 会议；due to 由于；unexpected 意外的；absence 缺席；keynote speaker 主讲人。\n【选项】A. put off 推迟——符合\"因故延期会议\"的语境，正确。B. put away 收好、放好——语义不符，故不选。C. put out 扑灭（火）、关掉——语义不符，故不选。D. put up 张贴、搭建——语义不符，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-7","origNo":7,"type":"single","stem":"He is a very ______ person and never gets angry even when others are rude to him.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"patient"},{"key":"B","text":"confident"},{"key":"C","text":"tolerant"},{"key":"D","text":"modest"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：他是一个非常宽容的人，即使别人对他粗鲁也从不生气。\n【生词】rude 粗鲁的；get angry 生气。\n【选项】A. patient adj. 耐心的——侧重\"能等待、不急躁\"，与\"别人粗鲁也不生气\"侧重点不同，故不选。B. confident adj. 自信的——语义无关，故不选。C. tolerant adj. 宽容的、能容忍的——正合\"别人粗鲁也不发火\"的语境，正确。D. modest adj. 谦虚的——语义无关，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-8","origNo":8,"type":"single","stem":"The company is ______ new ways to reduce production costs without sacrificing product quality.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"looking after"},{"key":"B","text":"looking into"},{"key":"C","text":"looking for"},{"key":"D","text":"looking up"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：公司正在寻找在不牺牲产品质量的前提下降低生产成本的新方法。\n【生词】reduce 降低；production cost 生产成本；sacrifice 牺牲内脏（此处指\"牺牲、降低\"）。\n【选项】A. look after 照顾——语义不符，故不选。B. look into 调查——指深入调查某事，与\"寻找新方法\"语境略偏，故不选。C. look for 寻找——look for new ways（寻找新方法）符合语境，正确。D. look up 查阅、抬头看——语义不符，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-9","origNo":9,"type":"single","stem":"It is ______ that all students should arrive at the examination hall at least 15 minutes before the test starts.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"requested"},{"key":"B","text":"demanded"},{"key":"C","text":"required"},{"key":"D","text":"acquired"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：所有学生应在考试开始前至少15分钟到达考场，这是规定要求。\n【生词】examination hall 考场；at least 至少。\n【选项】A. request vt. 请求——语气较弱，多用于请求他人帮忙，不合硬性规定，故不选。B. demand vt. 要求——语气强，但多指主动提出要求，不合\"It is ___ that\"结构，故不选。C. require vt. 要求、规定——It is required that...（规定要求……）表硬性规定，为固定句型，正确。D. acquire vt. 获得——语义不符，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-10","origNo":10,"type":"single","stem":"She ______ her success to hard work and perseverance rather than to luck.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"contributes"},{"key":"B","text":"distributes"},{"key":"C","text":"attributes"},{"key":"D","text":"dedicates"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：她把自己的成功归因于努力和毅力，而不是运气。\n【生词】success 成功；perseverance 毅力；rather than 而不是；luck 运气。\n【选项】A. contribute v. 贡献——contribute...to...（对……作贡献），语义不符，故不选。B. distribute vt. 分发——distribute...to...（把……分发给……），语义不符，故不选。C. attribute vt. 把……归因于——attribute...to...（把……归因于……）符合\"把成功归于努力\"，正确。D. dedicate vt. 奉献——dedicate...to...（把……奉献给……），语义不符，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-11","origNo":11,"type":"single","stem":"I can’t ______ the difference between these two words, as they seem to have exactly the same meaning.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"tell"},{"key":"B","text":"say"},{"key":"C","text":"speak"},{"key":"D","text":"talk"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：我分辨不出这两个词的区别，因为它们似乎意思完全相同。\n【生词】difference 区别；exactly 完全地。\n【选项】A. tell vt. 辨别——tell the difference（辨别差异）为固定搭配，正确。B. say vt. 说——say the difference 不成立，故不选。C. speak v. 讲（语言）、说话——不合此处搭配，故不选。D. talk v. 谈话——不合此处搭配，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-12","origNo":12,"type":"single","stem":"The newly published book ______ a wide range of topics, from history to modern technology.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"covers"},{"key":"B","text":"contains"},{"key":"C","text":"includes"},{"key":"D","text":"concludes"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：这本新出版的书涵盖了从历史到现代科技的广泛话题。\n【生词】newly published 新出版的；a wide range of 广泛的；topic 话题；modern technology 现代科技。\n【选项】A. cover vt. 涵盖、覆盖（范围）——书籍 cover 话题为常见搭配，正确。B. contain vt. 包含——强调内含某物，不用于\"涵盖话题范围\"，故不选。C. include vt. 包括——强调其中包括某些项，不合\"广泛话题范围\"的语境，故不选。D. conclude v. 总结——语义不符，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-13","origNo":13,"type":"single","stem":"The doctor ______ him to give up smoking for the sake of his health, but he just wouldn’t listen.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"persuaded"},{"key":"B","text":"advised"},{"key":"C","text":"suggested"},{"key":"D","text":"convinced"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意：医生为他的健康劝他戒烟，但他就是不听。\n【生词】give up 放弃（此处 give up smoking 戒烟）；for the sake of 为了；would not listen 就是不听。\n【选项】A. persuade vt. 说服——persuade sb to do sth 强调\"已说服成功\"，但后文\"他就是不听\"说明未成功，故不选。B. advise vt. 建议——advise sb to do sth（建议某人做某事）为固定搭配，符合\"医生劝告但他没听\"，正确。C. suggest vt. 建议——suggest 后不接 sb to do sth（应接 doing 或从句），结构不合，故不选。D. convince vt. 使信服——强调思想上的信服，搭配与语境不符，故不选。故选B。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-14","origNo":14,"type":"single","stem":"The witness’s account of the accident didn’t ______ with what the other witnesses had reported.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"agree"},{"key":"B","text":"correspond"},{"key":"C","text":"match"},{"key":"D","text":"compare"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意：这位证人对事故的陈述与其他证人报告的情况不一致。\n【生词】witness 证人；account 陈述、描述；report 报告。\n【选项】A. agree v. 同意、一致——agree with 也可表\"一致\"，但更常用于意见、食物，此处书面证词用 correspond 更贴切，故不选。B. correspond vi. 相符、一致——correspond with（与……相符）常用于陈述、证词等书面内容，正确。C. match vt. 匹配——多指\"相配\"（及物，不接 with 表一致），故不选。D. compare v. 比较——语义不符，故不选。故选B。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-15","origNo":15,"type":"single","stem":"The little boy showed great ______ in solving the difficult math problem all by himself.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"intelligence"},{"key":"B","text":"wisdom"},{"key":"C","text":"talent"},{"key":"D","text":"ability"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：这个小男孩独自解出了那道数学难题，表现出很高的智力。\n【生词】solve 解决；difficult 困难的；all by himself 全靠自己。\n【选项】A. intelligence n. 智力、聪明——show great intelligence（表现出很高智力），符合独自解难题的语境，正确。B. wisdom n. 智慧——多指人生阅历带来的睿智，与小孩解题不符，故不选。C. talent n. 天赋——多指某方面才艺天赋，不合此处，故不选。D. ability n. 能力——范围太泛，不如 intelligence 精准，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-16","origNo":16,"type":"single","stem":"The two countries have finally reached an ______ to cooperate in the field of environmental protection.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"agreement"},{"key":"B","text":"arrangement"},{"key":"C","text":"appointment"},{"key":"D","text":"approval"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：两国终于达成了一项在环保领域合作的协议。\n【生词】reach an agreement 达成协议；cooperate 合作；field 领域；environmental protection 环保。\n【选项】A. agreement n. 协议——reach an agreement（达成协议）为固定搭配，正确。B. arrangement n. 安排——语义不符，故不选。C. appointment n. 预约、任命——语义不符，故不选。D. approval n. 批准——语义不符，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-17","origNo":17,"type":"single","stem":"He is ______ to lose his temper when things don’t go his way, so you’d better be careful.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"likely"},{"key":"B","text":"probable"},{"key":"C","text":"possible"},{"key":"D","text":"liable"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：事情不顺他意时他很容易发脾气，所以你最好小心。\n【生词】lose one's temper 发脾气；go one's way 顺某人的意；had better 最好。\n【选项】A. likely adj. 可能的——也可表\"可能发脾气\"，但不强调性格上的\"易于动怒\"倾向，故不选。B. probable adj. 很可能的——不以人作主语，结构不合，故不选。C. possible adj. 可能的——不以人作主语，结构不合，故不选。D. liable adj. 易于……的——be liable to do sth（易于做某事）多指性格、脾气上的倾向，最贴合语境，正确。故选D。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-18","origNo":18,"type":"single","stem":"The old professor has ______ a large number of outstanding students who have made great contributions to society.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"trained"},{"key":"B","text":"educated"},{"key":"C","text":"cultivated"},{"key":"D","text":"brought"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：这位老教授培养了一大批对社会做出巨大贡献的优秀学生。\n【生词】a large number of 一大批；outstanding 优秀的；contribution 贡献。\n【选项】A. train vt. 训练——多指技能训练，不如 cultivate 贴合\"育人\"，故不选。B. educate vt. 教育——语义偏\"教育\"整体，不如 cultivate 精准，故不选。C. cultivate vt. 培养（人才）——cultivate students（培养学生）为常见搭配，正确。D. bring vt. 带来——bring up 才是\"培养\"，单独 bring 语义不符，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-19","origNo":19,"type":"single","stem":"The film was so ______ that many people in the audience were moved to tears.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"touched"},{"key":"B","text":"touching"},{"key":"C","text":"moved"},{"key":"D","text":"moving"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意：这部电影如此感人，以至于观众中许多人都被感动得落泪。\n【生词】audience 观众；be moved to tears 感动落泪。\n【选项】A. touched adj.（被动）被感动的——修饰人，但此处修饰 film（物），故不选。B. touching adj. 令人感动的——修饰物（film），与\"感动落泪\"呼应，正确。C. moved adj.（被动）被感动的——修饰人，此处修饰 film 不合，故不选。D. moving adj. 令人感动的——也可修饰物，但 touching 更侧重温情动人，与语境更贴切，故不选。故选B。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-vocab-20","origNo":20,"type":"single","stem":"It is reported that the government will ______ a new set of measures to deal with air pollution.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"adopt"},{"key":"B","text":"adapt"},{"key":"C","text":"adjust"},{"key":"D","text":"admit"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：据报道，政府将采取一套新措施来应对空气污染。\n【生词】report 报道；measure 措施；deal with 应对；air pollution 空气污染。\n【选项】A. adopt vt. 采纳、采取——adopt measures（采取措施）为常见搭配，正确。B. adapt v. 适应、改编——形近词，语义不符，故不选。C. adjust vt. 调整——语义不符，故不选。D. admit vt. 承认——语义不符，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"词汇"},{"id":"english-scene-1","origNo":1,"type":"single","stem":"— Hey, my favorite band’s playing at the campus festival next Friday! — Wait, _________ — confirm our group work division now?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Please turn over a new leaf"},{"key":"B","text":"Don’t pull our weight"},{"key":"C","text":"Let’s stick to the point"},{"key":"D","text":"You ticked all the right box"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：——嘿，我最喜欢的乐队下周五要在校园节上演出！——等等，我们还是说正事吧，现在确认一下小组作业的分工？\n【生词】favorite 最喜欢的；band 乐队；campus festival 校园节；stick to the point 紧扣主题、说正事；group work division 小组作业分工。\n【选项】A. Please turn over a new leaf（翻开新的一页、重新做人）——与语境无关，故不选。B. Don't pull our weight（别尽我们的本分）——语义不通且不合，故不选。C. Let's stick to the point（我们说正事吧）——对方偏离正题谈乐队，说话人想拉回小组作业话题，正确。D. You ticked all the right box（你各项都符合要求）——语义无关，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-2","origNo":2,"type":"single","stem":"— Good news! I’ve found a summer job! — _________. Anything interesting?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It’s a good idea"},{"key":"B","text":"You can’t be serious"},{"key":"C","text":"That’s great"},{"key":"D","text":"Check it out for yourself"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：——好消息！我找到一份暑假工作了！——太棒了。有什么有趣的吗？\n【生词】good news 好消息；summer job 暑假工作；interesting 有趣的。\n【选项】A. It's a good idea（好主意）——是对建议的回应，此处是分享好消息，不合，故不选。B. You can't be serious（你不是认真的吧）——表惊讶/怀疑，与下文\"有什么有趣的\"（感兴趣）矛盾，故不选。C. That's great（太棒了）——对好消息表示祝贺，且引出下文好奇提问，正确。D. Check it out for yourself（你自己去看吧）——语气冷淡，不合语境，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-3","origNo":3,"type":"single","stem":"— How did you find the film *Zootopia 2*? — _________. I can’t think too highly of it.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It makes no sense"},{"key":"B","text":"It’s up to you"},{"key":"C","text":"It’s awful"},{"key":"D","text":"It’s really amazing"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：——你觉得电影《疯狂动物城2》怎么样？——非常精彩。我再怎么称赞都不为过。\n【生词】How did you find... 你觉得……怎么样；can't think too highly of 对……评价极高（再怎么称赞都不为过）。\n【选项】A. It makes no sense（毫无意义）——与后文高度肯定矛盾，故不选。B. It's up to you（由你决定）——答非所问，故不选。C. It's awful（太糟了）——与后文矛盾，故不选。D. It's really amazing（非常精彩）——与\"再怎么称赞都不为过\"一致，正确。故选D。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-4","origNo":4,"type":"single","stem":"— Here is a new edition of *China Daily*. Do you want to have a look? — _________! Let me see it.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Don’t bother"},{"key":"B","text":"Not really"},{"key":"C","text":"By all means"},{"key":"D","text":"Mind your own business"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：——这是一份新版《中国日报》。你想看看吗？——当然可以！让我看看。\n【生词】edition 版本；have a look 看一看。\n【选项】A. Don't bother（别麻烦了）——表拒绝，与下文\"让我看看\"矛盾，故不选。B. Not really（不完全是/不怎么想）——表拒绝或否定，矛盾，故不选。C. By all means（当然可以、没问题）——表欣然接受，与\"Let me see it\"一致，正确。D. Mind your own business（管好你自己的事）——粗鲁无礼，不合语境，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-5","origNo":5,"type":"single","stem":"—Skipping breakfast is a good way to lose weight. —______. Actually it has the opposite effect.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"I’m glad to know that"},{"key":"B","text":"I can’t agree more"},{"key":"C","text":"That’s not the case"},{"key":"D","text":"That’s for sure"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：——不吃早餐是减肥的好办法。——并非如此。实际上它效果正好相反。\n【生词】skip breakfast 不吃早餐；lose weight 减肥；the opposite effect 相反的效果。\n【选项】A. I'm glad to know that（很高兴知道这个）——表示赞同，与后文\"效果相反\"矛盾，故不选。B. I can't agree more（我再同意不过了）——表完全同意，矛盾，故不选。C. That's not the case（并非如此）——礼貌反驳对方错误观点，与下文\"效果相反\"一致，正确。D. That's for sure（那是肯定的）——表赞同，矛盾，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-6","origNo":6,"type":"single","stem":"—You seem to be in a really good mood. —I finished my final examination today. ________! Now I’m free.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"What a coincidence"},{"key":"B","text":"What a relief"},{"key":"C","text":"I have got it"},{"key":"D","text":"I have had enough."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意：——你看起来心情很好。——我今天考完期末考试了。真让人松了口气！现在我自由了。\n【生词】in a good mood 心情好；final examination 期末考试；relief 松一口气；free 自由的。\n【选项】A. What a coincidence（真巧）——表巧合，与语境无关，故不选。B. What a relief（真松了口气）——考完试重获自由，心情轻松，符合\"心情很好\"，正确。C. I have got it（我懂了/我拿到了）——语义不符，故不选。D. I have had enough（我受够了）——负面情绪，矛盾，故不选。故选B。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-7","origNo":7,"type":"single","stem":"— I feel very thankful for your help when a serious accident happened to my brother yesterday. — ______. But luckily, he had a narrow escape.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Don’t worry about it"},{"key":"B","text":"Don’t overemphasize it"},{"key":"C","text":"Don’t take it seriously"},{"key":"D","text":"Don’t mention it"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：——昨天我哥哥出了严重事故，非常感谢你的帮助。——别客气。不过幸运的是，他死里逃生。\n【生词】thankful 感谢的；serious accident 严重事故；have a narrow escape 死里逃生、险些遇难。\n【选项】A. Don't worry about it（别担心）——回应\"担心\"而非\"感谢\"，不合，故不选。B. Don't overemphasize it（别过分强调）——表达生硬不自然，非礼貌回应，故不选。C. Don't take it seriously（别当真）——语义不合，故不选。D. Don't mention it（别客气、不用谢）——对感谢的礼貌回应，正确。故选D。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-8","origNo":8,"type":"single","stem":"— I plan to sign up for the school’s debate competition, but I’m worried about my public speaking skills. — ________. You’ve been practicing debates in class for months and you’re ready for it.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"I’d rather you didn’t"},{"key":"B","text":"That’s a bad idea"},{"key":"C","text":"You’ve got this"},{"key":"D","text":"Never mind"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：——我打算报名参加学校的辩论赛，但我担心自己的演讲能力。——你能行的。你在课堂上练了好几个月辩论，已经准备好了。\n【生词】sign up 报名；debate competition 辩论赛；public speaking 演讲；ready 准备好的。\n【选项】A. I'd rather you didn't（我宁愿你别报）——表反对，与下文鼓励矛盾，故不选。B. That's a bad idea（这是个坏主意）——表否定，矛盾，故不选。C. You've got this（你能行）——给予鼓励，与下文\"你已准备好\"一致，正确。D. Never mind（没关系）——用于安慰过失，答非所问，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-9","origNo":9,"type":"single","stem":"— Excuse me, could you tell me what you think about my proposal of organizing the campus book fair? I’ve prepared several plans but I’m not sure if they are practical… — ________. Just tell me the core idea of your plan and whether you have got the school’s approval or not.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Don’t beat around the bush"},{"key":"B","text":"I’m all ears"},{"key":"C","text":"You can say that again"},{"key":"D","text":"It’s a piece of cake"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：——打扰一下，你能说说对我组织校园书展这个提议的看法吗？我准备了好几个方案，但不确定是否可行……——别绕弯子了。直接告诉我你计划的核心想法，以及你是否已获得学校批准。\n【生词】proposal 提议；book fair 书展；practical 可行的；core idea 核心想法；approval 批准；beat around the bush 绕弯子、拐弯抹角。\n【选项】A. Don't beat around the bush（别绕弯子、直说吧）——对方绕来绕去，说话人希望直入主题，与下文\"直接告诉我核心想法\"一致，正确。B. I'm all ears（洗耳恭听）——表愿意倾听，与\"催促直说\"语气不符，故不选。C. You can say that again（你说得太对了）——表赞同，答非所问，故不选。D. It's a piece of cake（这很容易）——语义无关，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-10","origNo":10,"type":"single","stem":"—I think this contemporary abstract painting just looks like a mess of colours. —______. Actually, I find it quite meaningful.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"I beg to differ"},{"key":"B","text":"You read my mind"},{"key":"C","text":"I couldn’t agree more"},{"key":"D","text":"You’ve got me there"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：——我觉得这幅当代抽象画就像一团乱糟糟的颜色。——恕我不能苟同。实际上，我觉得它挺有意义的。\n【生词】contemporary 当代的；abstract 抽象的；a mess of 一团乱糟糟的；meaningful 有意义的；beg to differ 恕我不能苟同。\n【选项】A. I beg to differ（恕我不能苟同）——礼貌表达不同意见，与下文\"我觉得它挺有意义\"一致，正确。B. You read my mind（你读懂了我的心思）——表\"和你想的一样\"，与下文\"我不同意\"矛盾，故不选。C. I couldn't agree more（我再同意不过了）——表完全同意，矛盾，故不选。D. You've got me there（你把我难住了/你说到点子上了）——表被问住或承认对方有理，不合此处主动表态，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-11","origNo":11,"type":"single","stem":"—It’s a real dilemma. I can't decide which major to choose. Physics and literature both appeal to me. —______. But you should choose the one that you are most passionate about.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It’s a deal"},{"key":"B","text":"Never say never"},{"key":"C","text":"Don't mention it"},{"key":"D","text":"That's a tough call"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：——这真是个两难的选择。我决定不了选哪个专业。物理和文学都很吸引我。——确实难抉择。但你应该选你最热爱的那个。\n【生词】dilemma 两难抉择；major 专业；appeal to 吸引；passionate 热爱的；tough call 难做的决定。\n【选项】A. It's a deal（一言为定）——用于达成约定，答非所问，故不选。B. Never say never（别说绝不）——表\"世事无绝对\"，语义无关，故不选。C. Don't mention it（别客气）——回应感谢，不合，故不选。D. That's a tough call（确实很难抉择）——对对方两难处境表示理解，正确。故选D。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-12","origNo":12,"type":"single","stem":"The research team will _________ during the expedition (探险), as they must continue collecting data despite the approaching storm.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"rain cats and dogs"},{"key":"B","text":"brave the elements"},{"key":"C","text":"burn the midnight oil"},{"key":"D","text":"beat around the bush"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意：探险期间，研究队将顶风冒雨，因为尽管暴风雨逼近，他们仍必须继续收集数据。\n【生词】expedition 探险；collect data 收集数据；approaching 逼近的；storm 暴风雨；brave the elements 顶风冒雨、冒着恶劣天气。\n【选项】A. rain cats and dogs（下倾盆大雨）——描述下大雨，作谓语，不合此处\"面对恶劣天气\"语境，故不选。B. brave the elements（顶风冒雨）——符合\"暴风雨逼近仍继续工作\"，正确。C. burn the midnight oil（熬夜）——与天气无关，故不选。D. beat around the bush（绕弯子）——语义无关，故不选。故选B。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-13","origNo":13,"type":"single","stem":"—I stayed up all night cramming for the math test, and now I can’t remember a single formula! —Ugh, ________ when you try to memorize everything at the last minute. Next time, maybe study a little each day instead?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"that happens"},{"key":"B","text":"it turns"},{"key":"C","text":"this exists"},{"key":"D","text":"there arises"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：——我熬了一整夜突击数学考试，现在却一个公式都记不起来！——唉，临到最后一刻才想死记硬背，这种事常有。下次也许每天学一点？\n【生词】stay up 熬夜；cram 突击复习；formula 公式；memorize 死记硬背；at the last minute 临到最后一刻；that happens 这种事常有。\n【选项】A. that happens（这种事常有）——安慰对方\"临急抱佛脚记不住很正常\"，且引出建议，正确。B. it turns（结果……）——结构不完整，不合，故不选。C. this exists（这存在）——表达不自然，故不选。D. there arises（出现）——结构不合语境，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-14","origNo":14,"type":"single","stem":"— Say, what’s your favorite sport? — Hmmm... it’s hard to say. I like golf a lot — but I guess I like tennis better. — Do you play much tennis? — Yes, quite a bit. ______ — Sorry. I’m strictly a spectator — football, baseball, basketball, golf... I watch them all.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Where do you often spend your weekend?"},{"key":"B","text":"Who is your favorite player?"},{"key":"C","text":"Why don’t you do exercise regularly?"},{"key":"D","text":"How about a game sometime?"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：——你最喜欢的运动是什么？——很难说。我很喜欢高尔夫，但更喜欢网球。——你常打网球吗？——是的，常打。改天来比一场怎么样？——抱歉，我纯粹是个观众，足球棒球篮球高尔夫我都只看。\n【生词】spectator 观众；strictly 纯粹地。\n【选项】A. Where do you often spend your weekend?（你常在哪过周末？）——话题跳跃，与下文\"我只看球\"不衔接，故不选。B. Who is your favorite player?（你最喜欢的球员是谁？）——答语应是球员名，与\"我只看球\"不衔接，故不选。C. Why don't you do exercise regularly?（你为什么不经常锻炼？）——突兀质问，不合，故不选。D. How about a game sometime?（改天来比一场怎么样？）——谈论完网球后自然邀约比赛，引出对方\"我只看球不打\"的回答，正确。故选D。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-15","origNo":15,"type":"single","stem":"— Excuse me, is this seat taken? — No. it isn’t. — ______, so my friend and I can sit together? — No, not at all. — Thanks a lot.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Would you leave me alone for a while"},{"key":"B","text":"Would you mind getting out of my sight"},{"key":"C","text":"Would you mind moving over one"},{"key":"D","text":"Would my friend take your seat"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：——打扰一下，这个座位有人吗？——没有。——你介意往旁边挪一个位子吗，这样我和朋友就能坐一起？——一点也不介意。——多谢。\n【生词】Is this seat taken? 这个座位有人吗？sit together 坐一起；not at all 一点也不；mind 介意。\n【选项】A. Would you leave me alone for a while（你让我一个人待会儿好吗）——与\"想和朋友坐一起\"矛盾，故不选。B. Would you mind getting out of my sight（你介意从眼前消失吗）——粗鲁无礼，不合，故不选。C. Would you mind moving over one（你介意往旁边挪一个位子吗）——礼貌请求挪位，与下文\"不介意、谢谢\"衔接，正确。D. Would my friend take your seat（我朋友能坐你的位子吗）——直接占座不礼貌，且与\"挪一个\"逻辑不合，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-16","origNo":16,"type":"single","stem":"— What time is it? — It’s a quarter to five. — ______ — Five or five-thirty. He said it didn’t make any difference.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Jim is waiting for us — let’s hurry up!"},{"key":"B","text":"Where are we expected to meet Jim later?"},{"key":"C","text":"Jim is said to be with us at five tomorrow afternoon."},{"key":"D","text":"Aren’t we supposed to be at Jim’s house by five o’ clock?"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：——几点了？——差一刻五点。——我们不是该五点到吉姆家吗？——五点或五点半都行。他说没关系。\n【生词】a quarter to five 差一刻五点（4:45）；be supposed to 应该；make a difference 有影响/关系。\n【选项】A. Jim is waiting for us — let's hurry up!（吉姆在等我们，快走吧！）——若选此项，答语应是\"快点\"之类，而非讨论五点或五点半，故不选。B. Where are we expected to meet Jim later?（我们待会儿该在哪见吉姆？）——答语应是地点，与时间答语不合，故不选。C. Jim is said to be with us at five tomorrow afternoon.（据说吉姆明天下午五点和我们在一起。）——时态/时间与上下文不合，故不选。D. Aren't we supposed to be at Jim's house by five o'clock?（我们不是该五点到吉姆家吗？）——问完时间后讨论约定时间，引出\"五点或五点半都行\"，正确。故选D。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-17","origNo":17,"type":"single","stem":"— Haven’t I seen you somewhere before? — No, I don’t think so. — But you do look familiar. Wait a second... I know... We were on the same flight to New York last month. — Oh, yes. Now I remember. What a coincidence to meet in San Francisco! — Well, you know what they say — ______.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"it’s a small world."},{"key":"B","text":"you are so forgetful."},{"key":"C","text":"it’s the flight where stories begin."},{"key":"D","text":"you cannot step into the same river twice."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：——我们是不是在哪儿见过？——我觉得没有。——但你看着确实眼熟。等一下……我知道了……我们上个月坐的是同一班去纽约的航班。——哦，对。现在我想起来了。在旧金山碰到真是太巧了！——嗯，俗话说——世界真小。\n【生词】look familiar 看着眼熟；flight 航班；coincidence 巧合；it's a small world 世界真小。\n【选项】A. it's a small world（世界真小）——异地偶遇故人时常用的感慨，正确。B. you are so forgetful（你太健忘了）——指责语气，不合友好氛围，故不选。C. it's the flight where stories begin（故事始于这趟航班）——非通用俗语，故不选。D. you cannot step into the same river twice（人不能两次踏入同一条河）——讲变化，语义无关，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-18","origNo":18,"type":"single","stem":"— Excuse me. I wonder if you can help me. — Sure. What is it? — I want to have my hair cut, but I can’t find a barber shop. — I know where one is.______","options":[{"key":"A","text":"I’m not so sure."},{"key":"B","text":"Come on — I’ll show you."},{"key":"C","text":"It sells cool T-shirts."},{"key":"D","text":"You won’t take the wrong way."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意：——打扰一下，不知你能否帮个忙。——当然。什么事？——我想理发，但找不到理发店。——我知道有一家在哪。来，我带你去。\n【生词】have my hair cut 理发；barber shop 理发店；show you 带你去/指给你看。\n【选项】A. I'm not so sure（我不太确定）——与下文\"我知道有一家\"矛盾，故不选。B. Come on — I'll show you（来，我带你去）——主动带路，热情回应，正确。C. It sells cool T-shirts（它卖酷T恤）——说的是理发店，无关，故不选。D. You won't take the wrong way（你不会走错的）——未主动提供帮助，不如 B 热情，故不选。故选B。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-19","origNo":19,"type":"single","stem":"— Diana and I went for a drive in the country on Saturday. — ______. Where did you go?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"That sounds nice"},{"key":"B","text":"Not really"},{"key":"C","text":"Thank goodness"},{"key":"D","text":"Take it seriously"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：——星期六我和戴安娜去乡下兜风了。——听起来不错。你们去哪儿了？\n【生词】go for a drive 兜风；in the country 在乡下。\n【选项】A. That sounds nice（听起来不错）——对对方郊游表示赞赏，且引出下文\"去哪儿了\"的好奇，正确。B. Not really（不怎么样/并非如此）——表否定，与下文好奇提问矛盾，故不选。C. Thank goodness（谢天谢地）——用于庆幸化险为夷，不合，故不选。D. Take it seriously（认真对待）——语义无关，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-scene-20","origNo":20,"type":"single","stem":"-The team worked together and solved the technical problem in just two hours. -See? _____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Every cloud has a silver lining."},{"key":"B","text":"Too many cooks spoil the broth."},{"key":"C","text":"Many hands make light work."},{"key":"D","text":"The early bird catches the worm."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：——团队齐心协力，仅用两小时就解决了技术难题。——看见了吧？人多好办事。\n【生词】work together 齐心协力；technical problem 技术难题；many hands make light work 人多好办事。\n【选项】A. Every cloud has a silver lining（塞翁失马，焉知非福/黑暗中总有一线光明）——讲困境中仍有希望，不合，故不选。B. Too many cooks spoil the broth（人多反坏事/厨师多了煮坏汤）——讲人多反而搞砸，与语境相反，故不选。C. Many hands make light work（人多好办事）——讲团队合作使工作轻松，正合\"团队两小时解决难题\"，正确。D. The early bird catches the worm（早起的鸟儿有虫吃/捷足先登）——讲趁早行动，与团队合作无关，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"情景"},{"id":"english-gram-1","origNo":1,"type":"single","stem":"—Is your brother going camping? —He’s signed up but he’s considering________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"not to go"},{"key":"B","text":"not going"},{"key":"C","text":"won’t go"},{"key":"D","text":"not go"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意：——你哥哥要去露营吗？——他报名了，但他在考虑不去了。\n【生词】go camping 去露营；sign up 报名；consider 考虑。\n【考点】consider（考虑）后接动名词 doing，其否定形式为 not doing。\n【选项】A. not to go——consider 不接不定式，结构错误，故不选。B. not going——consider not going（考虑不去），否定动名词，正确。C. won't go——助动词将来时，不能作 consider 的宾语，故不选。D. not go——动词原形，不能作宾语，故不选。故选B。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-2","origNo":2,"type":"single","stem":"In nature some animals actively defend the area_________ they depend for the resources.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"in which"},{"key":"B","text":"on which"},{"key":"C","text":"which"},{"key":"D","text":"where"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意：在自然界中，一些动物会积极保卫它们赖以获取资源的领地。\n【生词】actively 积极地；defend 保卫；area 领地；depend on 依赖；resource 资源。\n【考点】定语从句修饰 area；动词短语为 depend on the area（依赖该领地），介词 on 提前，用\"介词 + which\"结构。\n【选项】A. in which——in 对应 depend in，搭配不存在，故不选。B. on which——对应 depend on，介词提前，正确。C. which——缺介词 on，故不选。D. where——关系副词，但此处需与 depend on 搭配，须保留 on，故不选。故选B。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-3","origNo":3,"type":"single","stem":"—What a terrible accident! —Yes. It happened __________ he four roads meet.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"where"},{"key":"B","text":"that"},{"key":"C","text":"when"},{"key":"D","text":"as"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：——多可怕的事故！——是的。事故发生在四条路交汇的地方。\n【生词】terrible 可怕的；accident 事故；meet 交汇。\n【考点】where 引导地点状语从句，意为\"在……的地方\"，说明事故发生的地点。\n【选项】A. where——引导地点状语从句\"在……的地方\"，正确。B. that——引导名词性/定语从句，不表地点，故不选。C. when——表时间，不合，故不选。D. as——表\"正如/当……时\"，不合，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-4","origNo":4,"type":"single","stem":"Mr. White, who I ______ to the south of France, still lives in London.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"think to have moved"},{"key":"B","text":"think had moved"},{"key":"C","text":"thought have moved"},{"key":"D","text":"thought had moved"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：怀特先生，我原以为他已经搬到法国南部了，现在仍住在伦敦。\n【生词】move 搬家；live in 住在；south of France 法国南部。\n【考点】主句谓语 thought 为过去时；move（搬家）发生在 thought 之前，即\"过去的过去\"，用过去完成时 had moved。\n【选项】A. think to have moved——think 用现在时与\"原以为\"（过去）矛盾，故不选。B. think had moved——think 时态错（应为过去时 thought），故不选。C. thought have moved——have 与过去时 thought 不能直接构成过去完成时，结构错误，故不选。D. thought had moved——thought（过去时）+ 宾语从句 had moved（过去的过去），正确。故选D。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-5","origNo":5,"type":"single","stem":"The officers narrowly escaped ___________ in the hot battle.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"have killed"},{"key":"B","text":"to kill"},{"key":"C","text":"to be killed"},{"key":"D","text":"being killed"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：军官们在激烈的战斗中险些丧命。\n【生词】officer 军官；narrowly escape 险些/勉强逃脱；hot battle 激战；kill 杀死。\n【考点】escape（逃脱）后接动名词 doing；officers 与 kill 为被动关系（被杀），用动名词的被动式 being killed。\n【选项】A. have killed——动名词形式错误，故不选。B. to kill——escape 不接不定式，故不选。C. to be killed——不定式被动，escape 不接不定式，故不选。D. being killed——动名词被动式，既满足 escape doing，又表被动，正确。故选D。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-6","origNo":6,"type":"single","stem":"The plan has to be ______ to meet the real situation.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"adjusted"},{"key":"B","text":"adapted"},{"key":"C","text":"produced"},{"key":"D","text":"refunded"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意：计划必须加以调整以符合实际情况。\n【生词】plan 计划；meet the real situation 符合实际情况。\n【考点】be adjusted to（被调整以适应……）；adjust 侧重\"调整（细节、计划）以符合\"。\n【选项】A. adjusted——be adjusted to（调整以适应实际情况），正确。B. adapted——adapt 侧重\"适应（环境）\"，不合此处\"调整计划\"，故不选。C. produced——生产，语义不符，故不选。D. refunded——退款，语义不符，故不选。故选A。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-7","origNo":7,"type":"single","stem":"_______about her ideal university, Jenny said she had no idea.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Ask"},{"key":"B","text":"When asking"},{"key":"C","text":"Asking"},{"key":"D","text":"When asked"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：当被问及理想大学时，珍妮说她还没想好。\n【生词】ideal university 理想大学；have no idea 不知道/没想好。\n【考点】Jenny 与 ask 之间是被动关系（被问），用过去分词表被动，When asked（当被问到时）。\n【选项】A. Ask——动词原形，不能作状语，故不选。B. When asking——现在分词表主动，与\"珍妮被问\"矛盾，故不选。C. Asking——现在分词表主动，矛盾，故不选。D. When asked——过去分词表被动，\"当被问到时\"，正确。故选D。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-8","origNo":8,"type":"single","stem":"I showed him the pictures I _______ of the animals the day before.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"was taken"},{"key":"B","text":"have taken"},{"key":"C","text":"had taken"},{"key":"D","text":"was taking"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：我给他看了我前一天拍的动物照片。\n【生词】show sb sth 给某人看某物；take pictures 拍照；the day before 前一天。\n【考点】\"拍照\"发生在\"showed（给他看）\"之前，即\"过去的过去\"，用过去完成时 had taken。\n【选项】A. was taken——被动（照片被拍），但此处指\"我拍的照片\"，主动，故不选。B. have taken——现在完成时，与主句过去时 showed 不一致，故不选。C. had taken——过去完成时，表\"过去的过去\"，正确。D. was taking——过去进行时，不合\"已完成\"之意，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-9","origNo":9,"type":"single","stem":"Look at the sign, Sir. We don’t permit ________ in the hall.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"smoke"},{"key":"B","text":"to smoke"},{"key":"C","text":"smoking"},{"key":"D","text":"to have a smoke"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：先生，请看标识。我们不允许在大厅里吸烟。\n【生词】sign 标识；permit 允许；hall 大厅。\n【考点】permit（允许）后接动名词 permit doing sth（允许做某事）。\n【选项】A. smoke——动词原形，不能直接作 permit 的宾语，故不选。B. to smoke——permit doing（而非 permit to do）作\"允许做某事\"解，故不选（注：permit sb to do 可，但此处无 sb）。C. smoking——动名词，permit smoking（允许吸烟），正确。D. to have a smoke——不定式，结构不合，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-10","origNo":10,"type":"single","stem":"Even the best writers sometimes find themselves________ for words.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"losing"},{"key":"B","text":"lost"},{"key":"C","text":"loses"},{"key":"D","text":"be lost"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意：即使是最优秀的作家有时也会发现自己一时语塞。\n【生词】even 即使；best writers 最优秀的作家；for words（找不到词）。\n【考点】find oneself + 过去分词表状态；be lost for words（一时语塞、说不出话），用过去分词 lost 作宾补。\n【选项】A. losing——现在分词表主动，\"作家让自己迷失\"不合被动状态，故不选。B. lost——过去分词表状态，find themselves lost for words（发现自己语塞），正确。C. loses——第三人称单数，不能作宾补，故不选。D. be lost——动词原形（be），不能作宾补，故不选。故选B。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-11","origNo":11,"type":"single","stem":"Most environmental problems exist because adequate measures for preventing them _______ taken in the past.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"had not been"},{"key":"B","text":"were not being"},{"key":"C","text":"were not"},{"key":"D","text":"was not"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：大多数环境问题之所以存在，是因为过去没有采取足够的预防措施。\n【生词】environmental 环境的；adequate 足够的；measure 措施；prevent 预防。\n【考点】measures 与 take 为被动关系（措施被采取）；时间状语 in the past 表一般过去时，用一般过去时的被动语态 were not taken。\n【选项】A. had not been——过去完成时被动，但 in the past 表一般过去时即可，无需\"过去的过去\"，故不选。B. were not being——过去进行时被动，不合，故不选。C. were not——were not taken（一般过去时被动），正确。D. was not——measures 是复数，was 不搭配，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-12","origNo":12,"type":"single","stem":"He ran as fast as he could _____ to catch the early bus.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"to hope"},{"key":"B","text":"hope"},{"key":"C","text":"hoping"},{"key":"D","text":"hoped"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：他尽可能快地跑，希望能赶上早班公交。\n【生词】as fast as he could 尽可能快地；catch the early bus 赶上早班公交。\n【考点】hoping（希望）作伴随状语，用现在分词表伴随动作，he 与 hope 为主动关系。\n【选项】A. to hope——不定式表目的，但此处是\"一边跑一边希望\"的伴随状态，故不选。B. hope——动词原形，不能与 ran 并列作状语（缺 and），故不选。C. hoping——现在分词作伴随状语，表主动伴随，正确。D. hoped——过去式，需与 ran 用 and 连接，否则结构错，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-13","origNo":13,"type":"single","stem":"You should be careful how you judge people ________ you cannot sum up a man's life in one moment.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"as though"},{"key":"B","text":"even though"},{"key":"C","text":"now that"},{"key":"D","text":"in that"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：你判断人时应谨慎，因为你不可能在一瞬间概括一个人的一生。\n【生词】judge 判断；sum up 概括；in one moment 一瞬间。\n【考点】in that（因为、在于）引导原因状语从句，解释\"应谨慎判断\"的理由。\n【选项】A. as though（仿佛）——表方式，不合因果，故不选。B. even though（即使）——表让步，不合，故不选。C. now that（既然）——表\"既然\"，语气与语境略偏，故不选。D. in that（因为、在于）——引导原因状语从句，解释为何要谨慎，正确。故选D。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-14","origNo":14,"type":"single","stem":"—I haven't heard from John for a long time. —What do you suppose ________ to him?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"was happening"},{"key":"B","text":"to happen"},{"key":"C","text":"has happened"},{"key":"D","text":"had happened"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：——我很久没收到约翰的消息了。——你认为他发生了什么事？\n【生词】hear from 收到……的消息；suppose 认为、猜测。\n【考点】询问到现在为止发生了什么，强调对现在的影响，用现在完成时 has happened。\n【选项】A. was happening——过去进行时，不合\"到现在为止\"，故不选。B. to happen——不定式，不能作从句谓语，故不选。C. has happened——现在完成时，表\"到现在为止发生的事\"，正确。D. had happened——过去完成时，需有过去参照点，此处无，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-15","origNo":15,"type":"single","stem":"____ at home, we can enjoy music or watch live sports games.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"To sit"},{"key":"B","text":"Sitting"},{"key":"C","text":"Sitted"},{"key":"D","text":"Being sitted"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意：坐在家里，我们可以欣赏音乐或观看体育直播。\n【生词】enjoy music 欣赏音乐；live sports games 体育直播。\n【考点】Sitting at home（坐在家里）用现在分词作状语，we 与 sit 为主动关系。\n【选项】A. To sit——不定式多表目的，此处是伴随状态，故不选。B. Sitting——现在分词作状语，表主动伴随，正确。C. Sitted——拼写错误（sit 的过去分词为 sat），故不选。D. Being sitted——sitted 非正确形式，故不选。故选B。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-16","origNo":16,"type":"single","stem":"You will find the laptop well-designed when he ______ it ______.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"will bring …back"},{"key":"B","text":"brings …back"},{"key":"C","text":"takes …away"},{"key":"D","text":"will take …away"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意：等他把笔记本电脑带回来，你就会发现它设计得很好。\n【生词】well-designed 设计得很好；bring back 带回来。\n【考点】when 引导时间状语从句，遵循\"主将从现\"原则（主句将来时，从句用一般现在时）。\n【选项】A. will bring ... back——从句不能用将来时，违反\"主将从现\"，故不选。B. brings ... back——从句用一般现在时，bring ... back（带回来）也符合语境，正确。C. takes ... away——take away（拿走），与\"能看到设计好\"逻辑矛盾，故不选。D. will take ... away——将来时 + 拿走，双重错误，故不选。故选B。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-17","origNo":17,"type":"single","stem":"If you apply yourself to ______ about the problem, you will soon solve it.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"think"},{"key":"B","text":"be thought"},{"key":"C","text":"thinking"},{"key":"D","text":"have thought"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：如果你专心思考这个问题，很快就能解决它。\n【生词】apply oneself to 专心于；solve 解决。\n【考点】apply oneself to 中 to 为介词，后接动名词。\n【选项】A. think——动词原形，不能作介词 to 的宾语，故不选。B. be thought——被动，不合\"自己思考\"，故不选。C. thinking——动名词，作介词 to 的宾语，apply yourself to thinking（专心思考），正确。D. have thought——完成式动名词，不合此处一般性陈述，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-18","origNo":18,"type":"single","stem":"There_______a sports meeting last week.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"be"},{"key":"B","text":"is"},{"key":"C","text":"was"},{"key":"D","text":"had"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：上周举行了一场运动会。\n【生词】sports meeting 运动会。\n【考点】There be 句型表\"存在/发生\"；时间状语 last week 表一般过去时；主语 a sports meeting 为单数。\n【选项】A. be——动词原形，不能与 There 直接连用表过去，故不选。B. is——一般现在时，与 last week 矛盾，故不选。C. was——There was（过去时，单数），正确。D. had——There be 不与 have 连用（There 不表\"拥有\"），故不选。故选C。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-19","origNo":19,"type":"single","stem":"The ceremony of Syria’s admission into the Belt and Road Initiative (BRI)________ in the capital Damascus on Jan. 12 and was attended by Fadi Khalil, head of the commission, who noted that Syria________ one of the main countries on the ancient Silk Road, particularly the cities of Aleppo and Palmyra.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"took place; had been"},{"key":"B","text":"was taken place; has been"},{"key":"C","text":"was taken place; was"},{"key":"D","text":"took place; was"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意：1月12日，叙利亚加入\"一带一路\"倡议的仪式在首都大马士革举行，委员会主席法迪·哈利勒出席了仪式；他指出，叙利亚曾是古丝绸之路上的主要国家之一，尤其是阿勒颇和巴尔米拉两座城市。\n【生词】ceremony 仪式；admission 加入；Belt and Road Initiative 一带一路倡议；capital 首都；attend 出席；commission 委员会；ancient Silk Road 古丝绸之路。\n【考点】第一空：take place（举行）无被动语态，用 took place；第二空：描述叙利亚过去\"曾是\"古丝绸之路主要国家，用一般过去时 was。\n【选项】A. took place; had been——第二空用过去完成时不必要，故不选。B. was taken place; has been——take place 无被动，故不选。C. was taken place; was——take place 无被动，故不选。D. took place; was——主动 took place + 过去时 was，正确。故选D。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-gram-20","origNo":20,"type":"single","stem":"What is it _____ his daughter needs most?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"what"},{"key":"B","text":"which"},{"key":"C","text":"that"},{"key":"D","text":"if"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意：他女儿最需要的是什么？\n【生词】need most 最需要。\n【考点】这是强调句型的疑问形式 What is it that...；强调句结构为\"It is/was + 被强调部分 + that...\"。\n【选项】A. what——强调句不用 what 引导，故不选。B. which——强调句不用 which，故不选。C. that——强调句疑问式 What is it that...，用 that，正确。D. if——不合强调句结构，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"语法"},{"id":"english-read-1","origNo":1,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\n“Kevin! Kevin! Did you hear what I said?” Ms. Reed, the band director,\nlooked straight at him — she wanted him to take Robin’s place, since\nRobin had broken her arm, and play a solo at the concert. Kevin agreed,\ntrying hard to hide his sadness: he was terrified of performing on\nstage, *freezing up* completely when playing alone, even though he was\nthe best saxophone player in his middle school band and played with the\nsenior high band too.\nJames, his bandmate, felt sorry for him and shared how he’d fainted on\nstage during a solo because he was so nervous. The story only made Kevin\nmore worried, but something worse was coming. Kelly, the overly\ncompetitive top flutist in the state, stopped him, saying that the Youth\nSymphony director would come to the concert to listen to his solo — and\nif he played well, he could get a chance to try out. Her words made him\nfeel heavy inside; he’d wanted to join the Youth Symphony for a long\ntime but never had the courage to audition.\nRunning out of the school, Kevin thought of excuses to skip the solo:\npretending to be ill, saying he’d hurt his finger. But he knew his\nparents would see he was lying, and hurting his finger on purpose was\ntoo dangerous. By the time he got home, his mom was very happy, talking\nexcitedly about the Youth Symphony chance and Ms. Reed’s praise. Kevin\nlied that his day was okay, then went down to the furnace room — called\n“Kevin’s Dungeon” — his practice space. He picked up his saxophone, took\na deep breath, and started to play.\nThis time, he didn’t focus on the fear of messing up; he focused on the\nmusic he loved. Maybe this solo wasn’t a nightmare — it was a chance to\nturn his dream into reality.\nThe final note faded, and loud applause burst out. Kevin smiled and felt\na warm rush of joy in his heart. He finally understood that courage\nisn’t the lack of fear, but going ahead in spite of it. Dreams never\ncome to those who run away from challenges; they always wait for those\nwho dare to face their fears and try.\n\n【题目】\nWhy did Ms. Reed ask Kevin to play the solo at the concert?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Kevin was the most skilled sax player in the senior high band."},{"key":"B","text":"Robin was unable to perform due to a broken arm."},{"key":"C","text":"The Youth Symphony director personally recommended Kevin."},{"key":"D","text":"Kevin had been eager to get the solo opportunity for a long time."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意（题目）：里德老师为什么让凯文在音乐会上独奏？\n【生词】solo 独奏；band director 乐队指挥；take one's place 代替某人；broken one's arm 摔断胳膊。\n【原文依据】第一段：\"she wanted him to take Robin's place, since Robin had broken her arm\"（她想让他代替罗宾，因为罗宾摔断了胳膊）。即罗宾因手臂受伤无法演出，里德老师才请凯文顶替。\n【逐项分析】A.（凯文是高中乐队最好的萨克斯手）错：原文说他是\"middle school band（中学乐队）\"里最好的，并非\"senior high band（高中乐队）\"。B.（罗宾因手臂受伤无法演出）对：对应\"Robin had broken her arm\"。C.（青年交响乐团总监亲自推荐凯文）错：总监是来\"听\"他独奏，并非推荐。D.（凯文一直渴望独奏机会）错：他一直想加入青年交响乐团但\"没勇气试音\"，并非渴望独奏。故选B。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-2","origNo":2,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\n“Kevin! Kevin! Did you hear what I said?” Ms. Reed, the band director,\nlooked straight at him — she wanted him to take Robin’s place, since\nRobin had broken her arm, and play a solo at the concert. Kevin agreed,\ntrying hard to hide his sadness: he was terrified of performing on\nstage, *freezing up* completely when playing alone, even though he was\nthe best saxophone player in his middle school band and played with the\nsenior high band too.\nJames, his bandmate, felt sorry for him and shared how he’d fainted on\nstage during a solo because he was so nervous. The story only made Kevin\nmore worried, but something worse was coming. Kelly, the overly\ncompetitive top flutist in the state, stopped him, saying that the Youth\nSymphony director would come to the concert to listen to his solo — and\nif he played well, he could get a chance to try out. Her words made him\nfeel heavy inside; he’d wanted to join the Youth Symphony for a long\ntime but never had the courage to audition.\nRunning out of the school, Kevin thought of excuses to skip the solo:\npretending to be ill, saying he’d hurt his finger. But he knew his\nparents would see he was lying, and hurting his finger on purpose was\ntoo dangerous. By the time he got home, his mom was very happy, talking\nexcitedly about the Youth Symphony chance and Ms. Reed’s praise. Kevin\nlied that his day was okay, then went down to the furnace room — called\n“Kevin’s Dungeon” — his practice space. He picked up his saxophone, took\na deep breath, and started to play.\nThis time, he didn’t focus on the fear of messing up; he focused on the\nmusic he loved. Maybe this solo wasn’t a nightmare — it was a chance to\nturn his dream into reality.\nThe final note faded, and loud applause burst out. Kevin smiled and felt\na warm rush of joy in his heart. He finally understood that courage\nisn’t the lack of fear, but going ahead in spite of it. Dreams never\ncome to those who run away from challenges; they always wait for those\nwho dare to face their fears and try.\n\n【题目】\nThe underlined phrase “freezing up” in Paragraph 1 probably means _________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"staying calm to deal with the performance"},{"key":"B","text":"feeling extremely cold on the frightening stage"},{"key":"C","text":"having great confidence in playing the solo"},{"key":"D","text":"being unable to move or act because of nervousness"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意（题目）：第一段中划线短语\"freezing up\"最可能的意思是？\n【生词】terrified 极度恐惧的；perform 表演；on stage 在台上；completely 完全地。\n【原文依据】第一段：\"he was terrified of performing on stage, freezing up completely when playing alone\"（他害怕上台表演，独奏时会完全 freezing up）。\"terrified（恐惧）\"提示这是紧张反应。\n【逐项分析】A.（保持冷静应对表演）错：与\"terrified（恐惧）\"相反。B.（在可怕舞台上感到极度寒冷）错：这是比喻，并非真觉得冷。C.（对独奏充满信心）错：与\"害怕上台\"矛盾。D.（因紧张而无法动弹）对：符合恐惧表演、僵住的语境。故选D。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-3","origNo":3,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\n“Kevin! Kevin! Did you hear what I said?” Ms. Reed, the band director,\nlooked straight at him — she wanted him to take Robin’s place, since\nRobin had broken her arm, and play a solo at the concert. Kevin agreed,\ntrying hard to hide his sadness: he was terrified of performing on\nstage, *freezing up* completely when playing alone, even though he was\nthe best saxophone player in his middle school band and played with the\nsenior high band too.\nJames, his bandmate, felt sorry for him and shared how he’d fainted on\nstage during a solo because he was so nervous. The story only made Kevin\nmore worried, but something worse was coming. Kelly, the overly\ncompetitive top flutist in the state, stopped him, saying that the Youth\nSymphony director would come to the concert to listen to his solo — and\nif he played well, he could get a chance to try out. Her words made him\nfeel heavy inside; he’d wanted to join the Youth Symphony for a long\ntime but never had the courage to audition.\nRunning out of the school, Kevin thought of excuses to skip the solo:\npretending to be ill, saying he’d hurt his finger. But he knew his\nparents would see he was lying, and hurting his finger on purpose was\ntoo dangerous. By the time he got home, his mom was very happy, talking\nexcitedly about the Youth Symphony chance and Ms. Reed’s praise. Kevin\nlied that his day was okay, then went down to the furnace room — called\n“Kevin’s Dungeon” — his practice space. He picked up his saxophone, took\na deep breath, and started to play.\nThis time, he didn’t focus on the fear of messing up; he focused on the\nmusic he loved. Maybe this solo wasn’t a nightmare — it was a chance to\nturn his dream into reality.\nThe final note faded, and loud applause burst out. Kevin smiled and felt\na warm rush of joy in his heart. He finally understood that courage\nisn’t the lack of fear, but going ahead in spite of it. Dreams never\ncome to those who run away from challenges; they always wait for those\nwho dare to face their fears and try.\n\n【题目】\nWhat made Kevin’s anxiety grow after he received the solo task?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"James told him about his own embarrassing solo experience."},{"key":"B","text":"His mother didn’t believe he could perform well on stage."},{"key":"C","text":"Kelly laughed at him for lacking the courage to audition."},{"key":"D","text":"He found his practice room was too small to play the saxophone."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意（题目）：凯文接到独奏任务后，是什么让他的焦虑加剧了？\n【生词】anxiety 焦虑；grow 加剧；receive 接到；faint 晕倒；nervous 紧张。\n【原文依据】第二段：\"James... shared how he'd fainted on stage during a solo because he was so nervous. The story only made Kevin more worried\"（詹姆斯讲述自己因太紧张在独奏时晕倒在台上的经历，这让凯文更担心了）。\n【逐项分析】A.（詹姆斯讲述了自己尴尬的独奏经历）对：对应\"詹姆斯晕倒的经历让凯文更担忧\"。B.（母亲不相信他能表演好）错：原文母亲\"非常高兴\"，并未质疑。C.（凯利嘲笑他没勇气试音）错：凯利是告诉他总监要来听，并未嘲笑。D.（发现练习室太小）错：原文未提练习室太小。故选A。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-4","origNo":4,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\n“Kevin! Kevin! Did you hear what I said?” Ms. Reed, the band director,\nlooked straight at him — she wanted him to take Robin’s place, since\nRobin had broken her arm, and play a solo at the concert. Kevin agreed,\ntrying hard to hide his sadness: he was terrified of performing on\nstage, *freezing up* completely when playing alone, even though he was\nthe best saxophone player in his middle school band and played with the\nsenior high band too.\nJames, his bandmate, felt sorry for him and shared how he’d fainted on\nstage during a solo because he was so nervous. The story only made Kevin\nmore worried, but something worse was coming. Kelly, the overly\ncompetitive top flutist in the state, stopped him, saying that the Youth\nSymphony director would come to the concert to listen to his solo — and\nif he played well, he could get a chance to try out. Her words made him\nfeel heavy inside; he’d wanted to join the Youth Symphony for a long\ntime but never had the courage to audition.\nRunning out of the school, Kevin thought of excuses to skip the solo:\npretending to be ill, saying he’d hurt his finger. But he knew his\nparents would see he was lying, and hurting his finger on purpose was\ntoo dangerous. By the time he got home, his mom was very happy, talking\nexcitedly about the Youth Symphony chance and Ms. Reed’s praise. Kevin\nlied that his day was okay, then went down to the furnace room — called\n“Kevin’s Dungeon” — his practice space. He picked up his saxophone, took\na deep breath, and started to play.\nThis time, he didn’t focus on the fear of messing up; he focused on the\nmusic he loved. Maybe this solo wasn’t a nightmare — it was a chance to\nturn his dream into reality.\nThe final note faded, and loud applause burst out. Kevin smiled and felt\na warm rush of joy in his heart. He finally understood that courage\nisn’t the lack of fear, but going ahead in spite of it. Dreams never\ncome to those who run away from challenges; they always wait for those\nwho dare to face their fears and try.\n\n【题目】\nWe can infer from the last paragraph that Kevin finally chose to _________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"pretend to be sick and miss the concert instantly"},{"key":"B","text":"gave up his dream of joining the Youth Symphony"},{"key":"C","text":"face the challenge and practice for the solo actively"},{"key":"D","text":"ask the band director to replace him with another student"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意（题目）：从最后一段可推断凯文最终选择了什么？\n【生词】infer 推断；finally 最终；choose 选择；pretend 假装；give up 放弃；replace 替换。\n【原文依据】第三、四段：\"He picked up his saxophone, took a deep breath, and started to play... he focused on the music he loved... a chance to turn his dream into reality\"（他拿起萨克斯、深呼吸、开始吹奏，专注于自己热爱的音乐，把独奏视为实现梦想的机会）。\n【逐项分析】A.（装病立刻错过音乐会）错：他曾想过但最终没做。B.（放弃加入青年交响乐团的梦想）错：他反而把它当机会。C.（面对挑战、积极练习独奏）对：对应他拿起萨克斯练习。D.（请指挥换人）错：原文未提，他是自己面对的。故选C。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-5","origNo":5,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\n“Kevin! Kevin! Did you hear what I said?” Ms. Reed, the band director,\nlooked straight at him — she wanted him to take Robin’s place, since\nRobin had broken her arm, and play a solo at the concert. Kevin agreed,\ntrying hard to hide his sadness: he was terrified of performing on\nstage, *freezing up* completely when playing alone, even though he was\nthe best saxophone player in his middle school band and played with the\nsenior high band too.\nJames, his bandmate, felt sorry for him and shared how he’d fainted on\nstage during a solo because he was so nervous. The story only made Kevin\nmore worried, but something worse was coming. Kelly, the overly\ncompetitive top flutist in the state, stopped him, saying that the Youth\nSymphony director would come to the concert to listen to his solo — and\nif he played well, he could get a chance to try out. Her words made him\nfeel heavy inside; he’d wanted to join the Youth Symphony for a long\ntime but never had the courage to audition.\nRunning out of the school, Kevin thought of excuses to skip the solo:\npretending to be ill, saying he’d hurt his finger. But he knew his\nparents would see he was lying, and hurting his finger on purpose was\ntoo dangerous. By the time he got home, his mom was very happy, talking\nexcitedly about the Youth Symphony chance and Ms. Reed’s praise. Kevin\nlied that his day was okay, then went down to the furnace room — called\n“Kevin’s Dungeon” — his practice space. He picked up his saxophone, took\na deep breath, and started to play.\nThis time, he didn’t focus on the fear of messing up; he focused on the\nmusic he loved. Maybe this solo wasn’t a nightmare — it was a chance to\nturn his dream into reality.\nThe final note faded, and loud applause burst out. Kevin smiled and felt\na warm rush of joy in his heart. He finally understood that courage\nisn’t the lack of fear, but going ahead in spite of it. Dreams never\ncome to those who run away from challenges; they always wait for those\nwho dare to face their fears and try.\n\n【题目】\nWhat is the best title for the passage?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"A Successful Solo Performance"},{"key":"B","text":"The Competitive Flutist Kelly"},{"key":"C","text":"A Strict, Generous and Kind Band Director"},{"key":"D","text":"Stage Fright and A New Chance"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意（题目）：本文最合适的标题是？\n【生词】title 标题；stage fright 舞台恐惧；a new chance 一个新机会；competitive 好胜的；flutist 长笛手；strict 严格的；generous 慷慨的；kind 善良的。\n【原文依据】全文围绕凯文\"害怕上台（stage fright）\"展开，最终他抓住独奏这个\"新机会\"克服恐惧；末段点题：\"courage isn't the lack of fear, but going ahead in spite of it\"（勇气不是没有恐惧，而是带着恐惧前行）。\n【逐项分析】A.（一场成功的独奏演出）错：只覆盖结尾，未涵盖克服恐惧的主线。B.（好胜的长笛手凯利）错：凯利只是配角。C.（严格慷慨善良的乐队指挥）错：里德老师着墨不多，非主角。D.（舞台恐惧与新机会）对：既点出冲突又点出转折，最能概括全文。故选D。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-6","origNo":6,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nThe 2026 CCTV Spring Festival Gala featured a breathtaking performance\nthat seamlessly blended traditional Chinese martial arts with\ncutting-edge technology: a carefully designed martial arts routine\nperformed by humanoid robots. This was not merely entertainment for the\naudience; it was a powerful declaration of China’s rapid and impressive\nadvancements in the fields of robotics and artificial intelligence.\nCompared to their debut just a year earlier, the 2026 robots\ndemonstrated astonishing progress in every aspect. Gone were the stiff,\nawkward and hesitant movements of the past. Dozens of Unitree’s G1\nhumanoid robots perfectly carried out complex and skillful actions,\nincluding high-speed flips, synchronized (同步的) nunchaku swings, and\neven the delicate, unsteady steps of “Drunken Fist.” They maintained\nstable balance, performed flawless formations, and interacted safely\nwith young human martial artists, a breakthrough that reflected huge\nleaps in both stability and dynamic motion control.\nBehind the striking performance lies a *convergence* of advanced\ntechnologies that together make such precise movements possible. Powered\nby highly developed AI control systems and equipped with LiDAR\n(激光雷达) sensors, the robots can map the 3D stage environment in real\ntime, enabling them to recognize their positions accurately and adjust\ntheir movements instantly to stay perfectly synchronized. Their flexible\njumps and flips rely on powerful actuators (执行器) and massive\nprofessional training data, proving that machines can now attain a level\nof athleticism once considered unique to humans.\nThe meaning of the display goes far beyond artistic effect. As a\nmilestone in intelligent manufacturing, the show underlines China’s\ngrowing competitiveness in the global humanoid robot industry. While\nChinese companies take a major share of the world market, the gala\nperformance more importantly shows the nation’s strong ability to\ncombine AI technology with a complete supply chain. It suggests a future\nwhere robots, having mastered the precision of kung fu, will undertake\ncomplex tasks in industry and daily life, turning technological strength\ninto practical social value.\n\n【题目】\nWhat was the most significant improvement in the 2026 robot performance compared to the previous year?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The robots performed hesitant movements on a larger stage."},{"key":"B","text":"The robots moved more smoothly and performed complex actions."},{"key":"C","text":"The robots were controlled by young human performers."},{"key":"D","text":"The robots could speak and interact with the martial audience."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意（题目）：与上一年相比，2026年机器人表演最重大的改进是什么？\n【生词】significant 重大的；improvement 改进；compared to 与……相比；previous 先前的；smoothly 流畅地；complex 复杂的。\n【原文依据】第二段：\"Gone were the stiff, awkward and hesitant movements of the past... perfectly carried out complex and skillful actions\"（过去僵硬、笨拙、迟疑的动作不见了……完美完成了复杂精巧的动作）。\n【逐项分析】A.（机器人在更大舞台上表演迟疑动作）错：僵硬迟疑的动作\"已消失\"。B.（机器人动作更流畅、能完成复杂动作）对：对应原文。C.（机器人由人类表演者控制）错：是与人类\"安全互动\"，非被控制。D.（机器人能说话）错：原文未提。故选B。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-7","origNo":7,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nThe 2026 CCTV Spring Festival Gala featured a breathtaking performance\nthat seamlessly blended traditional Chinese martial arts with\ncutting-edge technology: a carefully designed martial arts routine\nperformed by humanoid robots. This was not merely entertainment for the\naudience; it was a powerful declaration of China’s rapid and impressive\nadvancements in the fields of robotics and artificial intelligence.\nCompared to their debut just a year earlier, the 2026 robots\ndemonstrated astonishing progress in every aspect. Gone were the stiff,\nawkward and hesitant movements of the past. Dozens of Unitree’s G1\nhumanoid robots perfectly carried out complex and skillful actions,\nincluding high-speed flips, synchronized (同步的) nunchaku swings, and\neven the delicate, unsteady steps of “Drunken Fist.” They maintained\nstable balance, performed flawless formations, and interacted safely\nwith young human martial artists, a breakthrough that reflected huge\nleaps in both stability and dynamic motion control.\nBehind the striking performance lies a *convergence* of advanced\ntechnologies that together make such precise movements possible. Powered\nby highly developed AI control systems and equipped with LiDAR\n(激光雷达) sensors, the robots can map the 3D stage environment in real\ntime, enabling them to recognize their positions accurately and adjust\ntheir movements instantly to stay perfectly synchronized. Their flexible\njumps and flips rely on powerful actuators (执行器) and massive\nprofessional training data, proving that machines can now attain a level\nof athleticism once considered unique to humans.\nThe meaning of the display goes far beyond artistic effect. As a\nmilestone in intelligent manufacturing, the show underlines China’s\ngrowing competitiveness in the global humanoid robot industry. While\nChinese companies take a major share of the world market, the gala\nperformance more importantly shows the nation’s strong ability to\ncombine AI technology with a complete supply chain. It suggests a future\nwhere robots, having mastered the precision of kung fu, will undertake\ncomplex tasks in industry and daily life, turning technological strength\ninto practical social value.\n\n【题目】\nThe underlined word “convergence” in Paragraph 3 most probably means _________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"combination"},{"key":"B","text":"competition"},{"key":"C","text":"conflict"},{"key":"D","text":"creativity"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意（题目）：第三段中划线词\"convergence\"最可能的意思是？\n【生词】underlined 划线的；advanced 先进的；precise 精准的；combination 结合/融合；competition 竞争；conflict 冲突；creativity 创造力。\n【原文依据】第三段：\"a convergence of advanced technologies that together make such precise movements possible\"（先进技术的 convergence 共同使如此精准的动作成为可能）。关键线索\"together（共同）\"说明多种技术合在一起。\n【逐项分析】A. combination（结合/融合）对：与\"together（共同协作）\"一致。B. competition（竞争）错：与\"共同\"相反。C. conflict（冲突）错：与\"共同协作\"相反。D. creativity（创造力）错：此处强调\"结合\"而非新颖。故选A。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-8","origNo":8,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nThe 2026 CCTV Spring Festival Gala featured a breathtaking performance\nthat seamlessly blended traditional Chinese martial arts with\ncutting-edge technology: a carefully designed martial arts routine\nperformed by humanoid robots. This was not merely entertainment for the\naudience; it was a powerful declaration of China’s rapid and impressive\nadvancements in the fields of robotics and artificial intelligence.\nCompared to their debut just a year earlier, the 2026 robots\ndemonstrated astonishing progress in every aspect. Gone were the stiff,\nawkward and hesitant movements of the past. Dozens of Unitree’s G1\nhumanoid robots perfectly carried out complex and skillful actions,\nincluding high-speed flips, synchronized (同步的) nunchaku swings, and\neven the delicate, unsteady steps of “Drunken Fist.” They maintained\nstable balance, performed flawless formations, and interacted safely\nwith young human martial artists, a breakthrough that reflected huge\nleaps in both stability and dynamic motion control.\nBehind the striking performance lies a *convergence* of advanced\ntechnologies that together make such precise movements possible. Powered\nby highly developed AI control systems and equipped with LiDAR\n(激光雷达) sensors, the robots can map the 3D stage environment in real\ntime, enabling them to recognize their positions accurately and adjust\ntheir movements instantly to stay perfectly synchronized. Their flexible\njumps and flips rely on powerful actuators (执行器) and massive\nprofessional training data, proving that machines can now attain a level\nof athleticism once considered unique to humans.\nThe meaning of the display goes far beyond artistic effect. As a\nmilestone in intelligent manufacturing, the show underlines China’s\ngrowing competitiveness in the global humanoid robot industry. While\nChinese companies take a major share of the world market, the gala\nperformance more importantly shows the nation’s strong ability to\ncombine AI technology with a complete supply chain. It suggests a future\nwhere robots, having mastered the precision of kung fu, will undertake\ncomplex tasks in industry and daily life, turning technological strength\ninto practical social value.\n\n【题目】\nHow did the robots maintain perfect synchronization during the performance?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"They followed fixed routes without any adjustment."},{"key":"B","text":"They were remotely controlled by engineers behind the stage."},{"key":"C","text":"They used sensors to map the stage and adjust movements in real time."},{"key":"D","text":"They responded to voice commands from human performers."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意（题目）：机器人在表演中如何保持完美同步？\n【生词】maintain 保持；synchronization 同步；fixed routes 固定路线；adjustment 调整；remotely 远程地；voice command 语音指令。\n【原文依据】第三段：\"equipped with LiDAR sensors, the robots can map the 3D stage environment in real time... adjust their movements instantly to stay perfectly synchronized\"（配备激光雷达传感器，能实时绘制3D舞台环境……即时调整动作以保持完美同步）。\n【逐项分析】A.（走固定路线不调整）错：原文说\"即时调整\"。B.（后台工程师远程操控）错：靠AI和传感器自主完成。C.（用传感器实时映射舞台并调整动作）对：对应原文。D.（响应人类语音指令）错：原文未提语音指令。故选C。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-9","origNo":9,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nThe 2026 CCTV Spring Festival Gala featured a breathtaking performance\nthat seamlessly blended traditional Chinese martial arts with\ncutting-edge technology: a carefully designed martial arts routine\nperformed by humanoid robots. This was not merely entertainment for the\naudience; it was a powerful declaration of China’s rapid and impressive\nadvancements in the fields of robotics and artificial intelligence.\nCompared to their debut just a year earlier, the 2026 robots\ndemonstrated astonishing progress in every aspect. Gone were the stiff,\nawkward and hesitant movements of the past. Dozens of Unitree’s G1\nhumanoid robots perfectly carried out complex and skillful actions,\nincluding high-speed flips, synchronized (同步的) nunchaku swings, and\neven the delicate, unsteady steps of “Drunken Fist.” They maintained\nstable balance, performed flawless formations, and interacted safely\nwith young human martial artists, a breakthrough that reflected huge\nleaps in both stability and dynamic motion control.\nBehind the striking performance lies a *convergence* of advanced\ntechnologies that together make such precise movements possible. Powered\nby highly developed AI control systems and equipped with LiDAR\n(激光雷达) sensors, the robots can map the 3D stage environment in real\ntime, enabling them to recognize their positions accurately and adjust\ntheir movements instantly to stay perfectly synchronized. Their flexible\njumps and flips rely on powerful actuators (执行器) and massive\nprofessional training data, proving that machines can now attain a level\nof athleticism once considered unique to humans.\nThe meaning of the display goes far beyond artistic effect. As a\nmilestone in intelligent manufacturing, the show underlines China’s\ngrowing competitiveness in the global humanoid robot industry. While\nChinese companies take a major share of the world market, the gala\nperformance more importantly shows the nation’s strong ability to\ncombine AI technology with a complete supply chain. It suggests a future\nwhere robots, having mastered the precision of kung fu, will undertake\ncomplex tasks in industry and daily life, turning technological strength\ninto practical social value.\n\n【题目】\nWhat can be inferred from the last paragraph?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The performance was meant to show robots are better than humans at martial arts."},{"key":"B","text":"China has led the world in all fields of intelligent manufacturing."},{"key":"C","text":"Human performers will gradually disappear from traditional cultural galas."},{"key":"D","text":"The performance stood for China’s progress in AI and manufacturing integration."}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意（题目）：从最后一段可以推断出什么？\n【生词】infer 推断；underline 凸显；competitiveness 竞争力；industry 产业；integration 融合；manufacturing 制造业。\n【原文依据】末段：\"the show underlines China's growing competitiveness in the global humanoid robot industry... ability to combine AI technology with a complete supply chain\"（这场表演凸显了中国在全球人形机器人产业日益增强的竞争力……将AI技术与完整供应链结合的能力）。\n【逐项分析】A.（意在展示机器人武术比人类强）错：表演是展示技术融合。B.（中国在所有智能制造领域领先世界）错：过于绝对，原文只说\"竞争力增强\"。C.（人类表演者将逐渐消失）错：原文未提。D.（代表中国在AI与制造业融合上的进步）对：对应原文。故选D。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-10","origNo":10,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nThe 2026 CCTV Spring Festival Gala featured a breathtaking performance\nthat seamlessly blended traditional Chinese martial arts with\ncutting-edge technology: a carefully designed martial arts routine\nperformed by humanoid robots. This was not merely entertainment for the\naudience; it was a powerful declaration of China’s rapid and impressive\nadvancements in the fields of robotics and artificial intelligence.\nCompared to their debut just a year earlier, the 2026 robots\ndemonstrated astonishing progress in every aspect. Gone were the stiff,\nawkward and hesitant movements of the past. Dozens of Unitree’s G1\nhumanoid robots perfectly carried out complex and skillful actions,\nincluding high-speed flips, synchronized (同步的) nunchaku swings, and\neven the delicate, unsteady steps of “Drunken Fist.” They maintained\nstable balance, performed flawless formations, and interacted safely\nwith young human martial artists, a breakthrough that reflected huge\nleaps in both stability and dynamic motion control.\nBehind the striking performance lies a *convergence* of advanced\ntechnologies that together make such precise movements possible. Powered\nby highly developed AI control systems and equipped with LiDAR\n(激光雷达) sensors, the robots can map the 3D stage environment in real\ntime, enabling them to recognize their positions accurately and adjust\ntheir movements instantly to stay perfectly synchronized. Their flexible\njumps and flips rely on powerful actuators (执行器) and massive\nprofessional training data, proving that machines can now attain a level\nof athleticism once considered unique to humans.\nThe meaning of the display goes far beyond artistic effect. As a\nmilestone in intelligent manufacturing, the show underlines China’s\ngrowing competitiveness in the global humanoid robot industry. While\nChinese companies take a major share of the world market, the gala\nperformance more importantly shows the nation’s strong ability to\ncombine AI technology with a complete supply chain. It suggests a future\nwhere robots, having mastered the precision of kung fu, will undertake\ncomplex tasks in industry and daily life, turning technological strength\ninto practical social value.\n\n【题目】\nAccording to the passage, what future role are these robots expected to play?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Replacing all human workers in factories."},{"key":"B","text":"Performing only in entertainment shows."},{"key":"C","text":"Taking on complex tasks in industry and daily life."},{"key":"D","text":"Competing in international martial arts competitions."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意（题目）：据文章，这些机器人未来预计将扮演什么角色？\n【生词】future role 未来角色；expected 预计；replace 取代；factory 工厂；entertainment 娱乐；undertake 承担；industry 工业；daily life 日常生活。\n【原文依据】末段：\"robots... will undertake complex tasks in industry and daily life, turning technological strength into practical social value\"（机器人将在工业和日常生活中承担复杂任务）。\n【逐项分析】A.（取代工厂所有人类工人）错：\"所有\"过于绝对。B.（只在娱乐演出中表演）错：原文说意义\"远超艺术效果\"。C.（在工业和日常生活中承担复杂任务）对：对应原文。D.（参加国际武术比赛）错：原文未提。故选C。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-11","origNo":11,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nPersonal growth is often mistakenly regarded as a smooth upward path of\nachievements. Yet psychological research and real-life experiences\nconfirm a different truth: resilience — the ability to adapt and develop\namid difficulties  — is the unseen force driving deep and lasting\ngrowth. In an era of rapid changes and unexpected challenges, developing\nresilience is not just a survival skill but a key part of self-\ndevelopment, turning setbacks into stepping stones.\nThe link between difficulties and growth lies in the positive\npsychological changes after tough life events. Studies show that those\nwho deal with hardships rather than avoid them gain greater self-\nawareness, deeper understanding of others, and a stronger sense of\npurpose. For example, people who survive health problems with resilience\noften adjust their thinking, regaining confidence in their own abilities\nand valuing life more. This growth does not come automatically; it comes\nfrom facing difficulties on purpose, strengthening mental strength just\nas exercise builds muscle.\nResilience also plays an important role in life changes. Young people\nentering the workplace face setbacks such as rejected plans or\ndisagreements that cause self-doubt, but resilient people see these as\nlearning chances. They analyze their mistakes, ask for advice, and\ndevelop skills to make progress. Similarly, people traveling alone\nimprove resilience by dealing with unfamiliar environments on their own,\ntrusting their decisions and adapting to difficulties. Some people argue\nthat focusing on resilience puts pressure on people to ignore pain, but\nresilience means recognizing suffering without being controlled by it.\nIt is a skill that can be developed, not an inborn quality. Simple\nmethods such as changing negative thoughts, building support groups, and\npracticing thanksgiving can strengthen it over time.\nIn a society that values quick solutions, we must realize that true\ngrowth is built on resilience. Difficulties are an essential part of\ngrowth, not a detour. Developing resilience helps us face challenges\nbravely, adapt smoothly, and become stronger. It remains the most\nreliable companion on the path to meaningful personal growth.\n\n【题目】\nWhat is the common misunderstanding about personal growth according to the passage?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It requires great efforts to achieve."},{"key":"B","text":"It is a smooth process full of achievements."},{"key":"C","text":"It has a close connection with difficulties."},{"key":"D","text":"It can only be gained through work experience."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意（题目）：据文章，关于个人成长常见的误解是什么？\n【生词】common misunderstanding 常见误解；personal growth 个人成长；smooth 平坦的；upward 上升的；achievement 成就；connection 联系；work experience 工作经验。\n【原文依据】第一段：\"Personal growth is often mistakenly regarded as a smooth upward path of achievements\"（个人成长常被误认为是一条充满成就的、平坦的上升之路）。\"mistakenly（错误地）\"点明这是误解。\n【逐项分析】A.（需要付出巨大努力才能实现）错：这是作者认同的观点，非\"误解\"。B.（是顺利、充满成就的过程）对：对应\"被误认为平坦上升之路\"。C.（与困难密切相关）错：这是作者观点，非误解。D.（只能通过工作经验获得）错：原文未提。故选B。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-12","origNo":12,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nPersonal growth is often mistakenly regarded as a smooth upward path of\nachievements. Yet psychological research and real-life experiences\nconfirm a different truth: resilience — the ability to adapt and develop\namid difficulties  — is the unseen force driving deep and lasting\ngrowth. In an era of rapid changes and unexpected challenges, developing\nresilience is not just a survival skill but a key part of self-\ndevelopment, turning setbacks into stepping stones.\nThe link between difficulties and growth lies in the positive\npsychological changes after tough life events. Studies show that those\nwho deal with hardships rather than avoid them gain greater self-\nawareness, deeper understanding of others, and a stronger sense of\npurpose. For example, people who survive health problems with resilience\noften adjust their thinking, regaining confidence in their own abilities\nand valuing life more. This growth does not come automatically; it comes\nfrom facing difficulties on purpose, strengthening mental strength just\nas exercise builds muscle.\nResilience also plays an important role in life changes. Young people\nentering the workplace face setbacks such as rejected plans or\ndisagreements that cause self-doubt, but resilient people see these as\nlearning chances. They analyze their mistakes, ask for advice, and\ndevelop skills to make progress. Similarly, people traveling alone\nimprove resilience by dealing with unfamiliar environments on their own,\ntrusting their decisions and adapting to difficulties. Some people argue\nthat focusing on resilience puts pressure on people to ignore pain, but\nresilience means recognizing suffering without being controlled by it.\nIt is a skill that can be developed, not an inborn quality. Simple\nmethods such as changing negative thoughts, building support groups, and\npracticing thanksgiving can strengthen it over time.\nIn a society that values quick solutions, we must realize that true\ngrowth is built on resilience. Difficulties are an essential part of\ngrowth, not a detour. Developing resilience helps us face challenges\nbravely, adapt smoothly, and become stronger. It remains the most\nreliable companion on the path to meaningful personal growth.\n\n【题目】\nWhich of the following is NOT a benefit of dealing with hardships actively?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Gaining a better understanding of oneself."},{"key":"B","text":"Developing a stronger sense of purpose."},{"key":"C","text":"Avoiding all the pain in life."},{"key":"D","text":"Learning to value life more."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意（题目）：以下哪项不是积极应对困难带来的益处？\n【生词】benefit 益处；actively 积极地；deal with 应对；hardship 困难；self-awareness 自我认知；sense of purpose 目标感；avoid 避免。\n【原文依据】第二段：积极应对困难能带来\"greater self-awareness（更强自我认知）、deeper understanding of others（对他人更深理解）、stronger sense of purpose（更强目标感）、valuing life more（更珍惜生命）\"；第三段：\"resilience means recognizing suffering without being controlled by it\"（韧性是承认痛苦而不被其控制），并非消除痛苦。\n【逐项分析】A.（更好地理解自己）是益处，故不选。B.（更强的目标感）是益处，故不选。C.（避免生活中所有痛苦）对：与\"承认痛苦而不被控制\"矛盾，不是益处。D.（学会更珍惜生命）是益处，故不选。故选C。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-13","origNo":13,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nPersonal growth is often mistakenly regarded as a smooth upward path of\nachievements. Yet psychological research and real-life experiences\nconfirm a different truth: resilience — the ability to adapt and develop\namid difficulties  — is the unseen force driving deep and lasting\ngrowth. In an era of rapid changes and unexpected challenges, developing\nresilience is not just a survival skill but a key part of self-\ndevelopment, turning setbacks into stepping stones.\nThe link between difficulties and growth lies in the positive\npsychological changes after tough life events. Studies show that those\nwho deal with hardships rather than avoid them gain greater self-\nawareness, deeper understanding of others, and a stronger sense of\npurpose. For example, people who survive health problems with resilience\noften adjust their thinking, regaining confidence in their own abilities\nand valuing life more. This growth does not come automatically; it comes\nfrom facing difficulties on purpose, strengthening mental strength just\nas exercise builds muscle.\nResilience also plays an important role in life changes. Young people\nentering the workplace face setbacks such as rejected plans or\ndisagreements that cause self-doubt, but resilient people see these as\nlearning chances. They analyze their mistakes, ask for advice, and\ndevelop skills to make progress. Similarly, people traveling alone\nimprove resilience by dealing with unfamiliar environments on their own,\ntrusting their decisions and adapting to difficulties. Some people argue\nthat focusing on resilience puts pressure on people to ignore pain, but\nresilience means recognizing suffering without being controlled by it.\nIt is a skill that can be developed, not an inborn quality. Simple\nmethods such as changing negative thoughts, building support groups, and\npracticing thanksgiving can strengthen it over time.\nIn a society that values quick solutions, we must realize that true\ngrowth is built on resilience. Difficulties are an essential part of\ngrowth, not a detour. Developing resilience helps us face challenges\nbravely, adapt smoothly, and become stronger. It remains the most\nreliable companion on the path to meaningful personal growth.\n\n【题目】\nWhat can we infer from the passage about resilience?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It can be improved through certain methods."},{"key":"B","text":"It is a skill that only a few people can have."},{"key":"C","text":"It has nothing to do with life changes."},{"key":"D","text":"It makes people ignore their sufferings."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意（题目）：关于韧性，从文中可推断出什么？\n【生词】infer 推断；resilience 韧性；improve 提升；method 方法；inborn 天生的；quality 品质；suffering 痛苦。\n【原文依据】第三段：\"It is a skill that can be developed, not an inborn quality. Simple methods such as changing negative thoughts... can strengthen it over time\"（它是一种可以培养的技能，而非天生品质；改变消极想法等方法能随时间增强它）。\n【逐项分析】A.（可通过某些方法提升）对：对应\"可培养、可增强\"。B.（只有少数人拥有）错：原文说\"可培养\"，非少数人专属。C.（与人生变化无关）错：第三段说韧性\"在人生转变中也起重要作用\"。D.（让人无视痛苦）错：是\"承认痛苦而不被控制\"。故选A。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-14","origNo":14,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nPersonal growth is often mistakenly regarded as a smooth upward path of\nachievements. Yet psychological research and real-life experiences\nconfirm a different truth: resilience — the ability to adapt and develop\namid difficulties  — is the unseen force driving deep and lasting\ngrowth. In an era of rapid changes and unexpected challenges, developing\nresilience is not just a survival skill but a key part of self-\ndevelopment, turning setbacks into stepping stones.\nThe link between difficulties and growth lies in the positive\npsychological changes after tough life events. Studies show that those\nwho deal with hardships rather than avoid them gain greater self-\nawareness, deeper understanding of others, and a stronger sense of\npurpose. For example, people who survive health problems with resilience\noften adjust their thinking, regaining confidence in their own abilities\nand valuing life more. This growth does not come automatically; it comes\nfrom facing difficulties on purpose, strengthening mental strength just\nas exercise builds muscle.\nResilience also plays an important role in life changes. Young people\nentering the workplace face setbacks such as rejected plans or\ndisagreements that cause self-doubt, but resilient people see these as\nlearning chances. They analyze their mistakes, ask for advice, and\ndevelop skills to make progress. Similarly, people traveling alone\nimprove resilience by dealing with unfamiliar environments on their own,\ntrusting their decisions and adapting to difficulties. Some people argue\nthat focusing on resilience puts pressure on people to ignore pain, but\nresilience means recognizing suffering without being controlled by it.\nIt is a skill that can be developed, not an inborn quality. Simple\nmethods such as changing negative thoughts, building support groups, and\npracticing thanksgiving can strengthen it over time.\nIn a society that values quick solutions, we must realize that true\ngrowth is built on resilience. Difficulties are an essential part of\ngrowth, not a detour. Developing resilience helps us face challenges\nbravely, adapt smoothly, and become stronger. It remains the most\nreliable companion on the path to meaningful personal growth.\n\n【题目】\nWhat does the author think of difficulties in personal growth?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"They are unnecessary obstacles to avoid."},{"key":"B","text":"They are key parts of true personal growth."},{"key":"C","text":"They make people lose confidence easily."},{"key":"D","text":"They can only be solved with quick solutions."}],"answer":"B","explanation":"句意（题目）：作者认为困难在个人成长中处于什么地位？\n【生词】author 作者；difficulty 困难；unnecessary 不必要的；obstacle 障碍；avoid 避免；key part 关键部分；confidence 信心；quick solution 速效办法。\n【原文依据】末段：\"Difficulties are an essential part of growth, not a detour\"（困难是成长不可或缺的一部分，而非弯路）。\"essential（不可或缺的）\"是作者态度。\n【逐项分析】A.（应避免的不必要障碍）错：与\"不可或缺\"相反。B.（真正个人成长的关键组成部分）对：对应\"不可或缺的一部分\"。C.（让人轻易失去信心）错：与韧性使人更强矛盾。D.（只能用速效办法解决）错：作者反驳\"速效\"。故选B。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-15","origNo":15,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nPersonal growth is often mistakenly regarded as a smooth upward path of\nachievements. Yet psychological research and real-life experiences\nconfirm a different truth: resilience — the ability to adapt and develop\namid difficulties  — is the unseen force driving deep and lasting\ngrowth. In an era of rapid changes and unexpected challenges, developing\nresilience is not just a survival skill but a key part of self-\ndevelopment, turning setbacks into stepping stones.\nThe link between difficulties and growth lies in the positive\npsychological changes after tough life events. Studies show that those\nwho deal with hardships rather than avoid them gain greater self-\nawareness, deeper understanding of others, and a stronger sense of\npurpose. For example, people who survive health problems with resilience\noften adjust their thinking, regaining confidence in their own abilities\nand valuing life more. This growth does not come automatically; it comes\nfrom facing difficulties on purpose, strengthening mental strength just\nas exercise builds muscle.\nResilience also plays an important role in life changes. Young people\nentering the workplace face setbacks such as rejected plans or\ndisagreements that cause self-doubt, but resilient people see these as\nlearning chances. They analyze their mistakes, ask for advice, and\ndevelop skills to make progress. Similarly, people traveling alone\nimprove resilience by dealing with unfamiliar environments on their own,\ntrusting their decisions and adapting to difficulties. Some people argue\nthat focusing on resilience puts pressure on people to ignore pain, but\nresilience means recognizing suffering without being controlled by it.\nIt is a skill that can be developed, not an inborn quality. Simple\nmethods such as changing negative thoughts, building support groups, and\npracticing thanksgiving can strengthen it over time.\nIn a society that values quick solutions, we must realize that true\ngrowth is built on resilience. Difficulties are an essential part of\ngrowth, not a detour. Developing resilience helps us face challenges\nbravely, adapt smoothly, and become stronger. It remains the most\nreliable companion on the path to meaningful personal growth.\n\n【题目】\nWhat is the main idea of the passage?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The ways to deal with life’s difficulties."},{"key":"B","text":"The importance of quick solutions in modern society."},{"key":"C","text":"Resilience is the key driver of personal growth."},{"key":"D","text":"Different opinions on the role of resilience."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意（题目）：本文的主旨是什么？\n【生词】main idea 主旨；deal with 应对；quick solution 速效办法；modern society 现代社会；key driver 关键驱动力；opinion 观点；role 作用。\n【原文依据】全文反复论证：第一段\"resilience... is the unseen force driving deep and lasting growth\"（韧性是推动深度、持久成长的隐形力量），末段\"true growth is built on resilience\"（真正的成长建立在韧性之上）。\n【逐项分析】A.（应对困难的方法）错：只是第三段细节，非主旨。B.（速效办法的重要性）错：正是作者反驳的。C.（韧性是个人成长的关键驱动力）对：对应全文论点。D.（对韧性作用的不同观点）错：文中未展开对立讨论。故选C。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-16","origNo":16,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nWaking up just one hour earlier could reduce a person’s risk of major\ndepression by 23%, suggests a sweeping new genetic study. Previous\nobservational studies have shown that night owls are twice as likely to\nsuffer from depression as early risers, regardless of how long they\nsleep. In 2018, senior author Celine Vetter, assistant professor of\nintegrative physiology at CU Boulder, published a long-term study of\n32,000 nurses showing that “early risers” were up to 27% less likely to\ndevelop depression over the course of four years.\nTo get a clearer sense of whether shifting sleep time earlier is truly\nprotective, and how much shift is required, lead author Lyas Daglas, M.\nD．, turned to data from the DNA testing company and ten used a method\ncalled “*Mendelian randomization*” to decipher (破译) cause and effect.\nMore than 340 common genetic variants, including variants in the\nso-called “clock gene” PER2, are known to influence a person’s internal\nclock, and genes collectively explain 12-42% of our sleep time\npreference. The researchers assessed deidentified genetic data on these\nvariants from up to 850,000 individuals, including data from 85,000 who\nhad worn wearable sleep trackers for 7 days and 250,000 who had filled\nout sleep-preference questionnaires. In the largest of these samples,\nabout a third of surveyed people self-identified as early risers, 9%\nwere night owls and the rest were in the middle.\nWith this information in hand, the researchers turned to a different\nsample which included genetic information along with anonymized (匿名的)\nmedical and prescription records and surveys about diagnosis of major\ndepressive disorder. Using new statistical techniques, they asked: do\nthose with genetic variants which *predispose them to be early risers*\nalso have a lower risk of depression? The answer is a firm yes.\nEach-one-hour earlier sleep midpoint (halfway between bedtime and wake\ntime) corresponded with a 23% lower risk of major depressive disorder.\nThis suggests that if someone who normally goes to bed at 1 a.m. goes to\nbed at midnight instead and sleeps the same duration, they could cut\ntheir risk by 23%; if they go to bed at 11 p.m. they could cut it by\nabout 40%.\n\n【题目】\nWhat does the first paragraph mainly talk about?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The significant role of gene action in depression."},{"key":"B","text":"A new observational study about causes of depression."},{"key":"C","text":"The close relationship between depression and sleep time."},{"key":"D","text":"Whether early risers are less likely to suffer from depression."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意（题目）：第一段主要讲什么？\n【生词】first paragraph 第一段；mainly 主要；significant role 重要作用；gene action 基因作用；observational study 观察研究；cause 原因；relationship 关系；sleep time 睡眠时间。\n【原文依据】第一段核心句：\"night owls are twice as likely to suffer from depression as early risers\"（夜猫子患抑郁的概率是早起者的两倍）及\"early risers were up to 27% less likely to develop depression\"（早起者患抑郁风险最多低27%）。\n【逐项分析】A.（基因在抑郁中的作用）错：这是后文内容，第一段未展开。B.（关于抑郁成因的观察研究）错：第一段讲的是与\"睡眠时间\"的关联，非泛泛成因。C.（抑郁与睡眠时间的密切关系）对：对应第一段两条线索。D.（早起者是否更不易抑郁）错：只覆盖侧面，未涵盖\"夜猫子\"线索。故选C。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-17","origNo":17,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nWaking up just one hour earlier could reduce a person’s risk of major\ndepression by 23%, suggests a sweeping new genetic study. Previous\nobservational studies have shown that night owls are twice as likely to\nsuffer from depression as early risers, regardless of how long they\nsleep. In 2018, senior author Celine Vetter, assistant professor of\nintegrative physiology at CU Boulder, published a long-term study of\n32,000 nurses showing that “early risers” were up to 27% less likely to\ndevelop depression over the course of four years.\nTo get a clearer sense of whether shifting sleep time earlier is truly\nprotective, and how much shift is required, lead author Lyas Daglas, M.\nD．, turned to data from the DNA testing company and ten used a method\ncalled “*Mendelian randomization*” to decipher (破译) cause and effect.\nMore than 340 common genetic variants, including variants in the\nso-called “clock gene” PER2, are known to influence a person’s internal\nclock, and genes collectively explain 12-42% of our sleep time\npreference. The researchers assessed deidentified genetic data on these\nvariants from up to 850,000 individuals, including data from 85,000 who\nhad worn wearable sleep trackers for 7 days and 250,000 who had filled\nout sleep-preference questionnaires. In the largest of these samples,\nabout a third of surveyed people self-identified as early risers, 9%\nwere night owls and the rest were in the middle.\nWith this information in hand, the researchers turned to a different\nsample which included genetic information along with anonymized (匿名的)\nmedical and prescription records and surveys about diagnosis of major\ndepressive disorder. Using new statistical techniques, they asked: do\nthose with genetic variants which *predispose them to be early risers*\nalso have a lower risk of depression? The answer is a firm yes.\nEach-one-hour earlier sleep midpoint (halfway between bedtime and wake\ntime) corresponded with a 23% lower risk of major depressive disorder.\nThis suggests that if someone who normally goes to bed at 1 a.m. goes to\nbed at midnight instead and sleeps the same duration, they could cut\ntheir risk by 23%; if they go to bed at 11 p.m. they could cut it by\nabout 40%.\n\n【题目】\nHow did Lyas Daghlas decipher cause and effect?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"By exploring the influence of genes on depression."},{"key":"B","text":"By analyzing samples of surveyed people’s self-identification."},{"key":"C","text":"By tracking individuals diagnosed with depressive disorder."},{"key":"D","text":"By proving the decisive role sleep time plays in genetic variants."}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意（题目）：莱亚斯·达格拉斯是如何厘清因果关系的？\n【生词】decipher 破译、厘清；cause and effect 因果；explore 探究；influence 影响；sample 样本；self-identification 自我认定；track 追踪；diagnose 诊断；decisive 决定性的；variant 变异。\n【原文依据】第二段：他借助DNA数据\"used a method called Mendelian randomization to decipher cause and effect\"（用孟德尔随机化方法破译因果），并指出\"More than 340 common genetic variants... influence a person's internal clock\"（340多种基因变异影响人体生物钟）。\n【逐项分析】A.（通过探究基因对抑郁的影响）对：他用基因数据探究基因变异与抑郁风险的关系。B.（分析自我认同样本）错：用的是基因数据。C.（追踪确诊患者）错：追踪的是基因变异与风险，非患者。D.（证明睡眠时间对基因变异起决定作用）错：因果方向恰相反。故选A。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-18","origNo":18,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nWaking up just one hour earlier could reduce a person’s risk of major\ndepression by 23%, suggests a sweeping new genetic study. Previous\nobservational studies have shown that night owls are twice as likely to\nsuffer from depression as early risers, regardless of how long they\nsleep. In 2018, senior author Celine Vetter, assistant professor of\nintegrative physiology at CU Boulder, published a long-term study of\n32,000 nurses showing that “early risers” were up to 27% less likely to\ndevelop depression over the course of four years.\nTo get a clearer sense of whether shifting sleep time earlier is truly\nprotective, and how much shift is required, lead author Lyas Daglas, M.\nD．, turned to data from the DNA testing company and ten used a method\ncalled “*Mendelian randomization*” to decipher (破译) cause and effect.\nMore than 340 common genetic variants, including variants in the\nso-called “clock gene” PER2, are known to influence a person’s internal\nclock, and genes collectively explain 12-42% of our sleep time\npreference. The researchers assessed deidentified genetic data on these\nvariants from up to 850,000 individuals, including data from 85,000 who\nhad worn wearable sleep trackers for 7 days and 250,000 who had filled\nout sleep-preference questionnaires. In the largest of these samples,\nabout a third of surveyed people self-identified as early risers, 9%\nwere night owls and the rest were in the middle.\nWith this information in hand, the researchers turned to a different\nsample which included genetic information along with anonymized (匿名的)\nmedical and prescription records and surveys about diagnosis of major\ndepressive disorder. Using new statistical techniques, they asked: do\nthose with genetic variants which *predispose them to be early risers*\nalso have a lower risk of depression? The answer is a firm yes.\nEach-one-hour earlier sleep midpoint (halfway between bedtime and wake\ntime) corresponded with a 23% lower risk of major depressive disorder.\nThis suggests that if someone who normally goes to bed at 1 a.m. goes to\nbed at midnight instead and sleeps the same duration, they could cut\ntheir risk by 23%; if they go to bed at 11 p.m. they could cut it by\nabout 40%.\n\n【题目】\nWhat is Lyas Daghals’s research mainly based on?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Medical treatment records of early risers."},{"key":"B","text":"The genetic research and data about DNA"},{"key":"C","text":"A comprehensive analysis of different samples."},{"key":"D","text":"250,000 people’s self-identification and questionnaires."}],"answer":"C","explanation":"句意（题目）：莱亚斯·达格拉斯的研究主要基于什么？\n【生词】research 研究；be based on 基于；medical treatment 医疗；record 记录；genetic 基因的；comprehensive 综合的；analysis 分析；questionnaire 问卷。\n【原文依据】第二、三段：研究综合了多个不同样本——多达85万人的基因数据（850,000 individuals）、8.5万人佩戴睡眠追踪器的数据（85,000 with wearable sleep trackers）、25万份问卷（250,000 questionnaires），第三段又\"turned to a different sample\"（转向另一个含基因信息与医疗记录的样本）。即把多个不同样本综合分析。\n【逐项分析】A.（早起者的医疗记录）错：医疗记录只是部分样本，且非只针对早起者。B.（基因研究和DNA数据）错：研究不止基因，还含睡眠追踪、问卷、医疗记录，B 太片面。C.（对不同样本的综合分析）对：对应多源多样本的综合分析。D.（25万人的自我认定和问卷）错：这只是众多样本之一。故选C。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-19","origNo":19,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nWaking up just one hour earlier could reduce a person’s risk of major\ndepression by 23%, suggests a sweeping new genetic study. Previous\nobservational studies have shown that night owls are twice as likely to\nsuffer from depression as early risers, regardless of how long they\nsleep. In 2018, senior author Celine Vetter, assistant professor of\nintegrative physiology at CU Boulder, published a long-term study of\n32,000 nurses showing that “early risers” were up to 27% less likely to\ndevelop depression over the course of four years.\nTo get a clearer sense of whether shifting sleep time earlier is truly\nprotective, and how much shift is required, lead author Lyas Daglas, M.\nD．, turned to data from the DNA testing company and ten used a method\ncalled “*Mendelian randomization*” to decipher (破译) cause and effect.\nMore than 340 common genetic variants, including variants in the\nso-called “clock gene” PER2, are known to influence a person’s internal\nclock, and genes collectively explain 12-42% of our sleep time\npreference. The researchers assessed deidentified genetic data on these\nvariants from up to 850,000 individuals, including data from 85,000 who\nhad worn wearable sleep trackers for 7 days and 250,000 who had filled\nout sleep-preference questionnaires. In the largest of these samples,\nabout a third of surveyed people self-identified as early risers, 9%\nwere night owls and the rest were in the middle.\nWith this information in hand, the researchers turned to a different\nsample which included genetic information along with anonymized (匿名的)\nmedical and prescription records and surveys about diagnosis of major\ndepressive disorder. Using new statistical techniques, they asked: do\nthose with genetic variants which *predispose them to be early risers*\nalso have a lower risk of depression? The answer is a firm yes.\nEach-one-hour earlier sleep midpoint (halfway between bedtime and wake\ntime) corresponded with a 23% lower risk of major depressive disorder.\nThis suggests that if someone who normally goes to bed at 1 a.m. goes to\nbed at midnight instead and sleeps the same duration, they could cut\ntheir risk by 23%; if they go to bed at 11 p.m. they could cut it by\nabout 40%.\n\n【题目】\nHow do you understand the underlined part in the last paragraph?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Make them more likely to rise early."},{"key":"B","text":"Change early risers’ habits."},{"key":"C","text":"Force them to change their chronotype"},{"key":"D","text":"Lead them to shift sleep time"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"句意（题目）：如何理解文中划线部分\"predispose them to be early risers\"？\n【生词】underlined 划线的；predispose 使……有倾向；likely 可能；rise early 早起；habit 习惯；force 强迫；chronotype 生物钟类型；shift 改变、调整。\n【原文依据】第三段：\"do those with genetic variants which predispose them to be early risers also have a lower risk of depression?\"（带有\"使他们倾向于早起\"基因变异的人，患抑郁风险是否更低？）。predispose 意为\"使……有倾向\"。\n【逐项分析】A.（让他们更可能早起）对：对应\"使他们倾向于早起\"。B.（改变早起者的习惯）错：偏离本义。C.（强迫他们改变生物钟类型）错：偏离本义。D.（引导他们调整睡眠时间）错：偏离本义。故选A。","chapter":"阅读"},{"id":"english-read-20","origNo":20,"type":"single","stem":"【阅读材料】\nWaking up just one hour earlier could reduce a person’s risk of major\ndepression by 23%, suggests a sweeping new genetic study. Previous\nobservational studies have shown that night owls are twice as likely to\nsuffer from depression as early risers, regardless of how long they\nsleep. In 2018, senior author Celine Vetter, assistant professor of\nintegrative physiology at CU Boulder, published a long-term study of\n32,000 nurses showing that “early risers” were up to 27% less likely to\ndevelop depression over the course of four years.\nTo get a clearer sense of whether shifting sleep time earlier is truly\nprotective, and how much shift is required, lead author Lyas Daglas, M.\nD．, turned to data from the DNA testing company and ten used a method\ncalled “*Mendelian randomization*” to decipher (破译) cause and effect.\nMore than 340 common genetic variants, including variants in the\nso-called “clock gene” PER2, are known to influence a person’s internal\nclock, and genes collectively explain 12-42% of our sleep time\npreference. The researchers assessed deidentified genetic data on these\nvariants from up to 850,000 individuals, including data from 85,000 who\nhad worn wearable sleep trackers for 7 days and 250,000 who had filled\nout sleep-preference questionnaires. In the largest of these samples,\nabout a third of surveyed people self-identified as early risers, 9%\nwere night owls and the rest were in the middle.\nWith this information in hand, the researchers turned to a different\nsample which included genetic information along with anonymized (匿名的)\nmedical and prescription records and surveys about diagnosis of major\ndepressive disorder. Using new statistical techniques, they asked: do\nthose with genetic variants which *predispose them to be early risers*\nalso have a lower risk of depression? The answer is a firm yes.\nEach-one-hour earlier sleep midpoint (halfway between bedtime and wake\ntime) corresponded with a 23% lower risk of major depressive disorder.\nThis suggests that if someone who normally goes to bed at 1 a.m. goes to\nbed at midnight instead and sleeps the same duration, they could cut\ntheir risk by 23%; if they go to bed at 11 p.m. they could cut it by\nabout 40%.\n\n【题目】\nWhich can be the suitable title for this text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Risk of Depression Decreases When Sleeping Time Increases"},{"key":"B","text":"Genes Have More Impact on Depression than Sleep Time Does"},{"key":"C","text":"A New Research Suggests Early Risers Carry Clock Gene called PER2"},{"key":"D","text":"Moving Sleep Time An Hour Earlier Could Cut Depression Risk by 23%"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"句意（题目）：哪个最适合作为本文标题？\n【生词】suitable title 合适标题；risk 风险；depression 抑郁；decrease 减少；sleeping time 睡眠时间；increase 增加；impact 影响；gene 基因；suggests 表明；cut 降低。\n【原文依据】核心结论（首段与末段）：\"Waking up just one hour earlier could reduce a person's risk of major depression by 23%\"（早醒一小时可使患重度抑郁风险降23%）；末段举例：把入睡时间从凌晨1点提前到午夜可降23%，提前到晚11点可降约40%。\n【逐项分析】A.（睡眠时间增加，抑郁风险下降）错：是\"提前睡眠时间\"而非\"增加时长\"，原文强调时长不变。B.（基因比睡眠时间影响更大）错：文章重点在睡眠时间。C.（新研究表明早起者携带PER2时钟基因）错：PER2 只是众多时钟基因之一，非主旨。D.（睡眠时间提前一小时可降低23%抑郁风险）对：准确概括全文核心结论。故选D。","chapter":"阅读"}]};
